QB.add(10,[{"id":10501,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Name the smooth layer labelled <b>A</b>, covering the articular end of the bone.","a":["articular cartilage","hyaline cartilage","hyaline articular cartilage"],"e":"A is articular (hyaline) cartilage covering the epiphysis in a synovial joint.","k":"A = articular cartilage.","img":{"src":"10/o_longbone","cap":"Adult long bone. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What is a facet?","What is a facet?","Small smooth articular surface covered with cartilage"],["","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Epiphysis"],["","What 3 things does a synovial joint have?","What 3 things does a synovial joint have?","Synovial membrane; Synovial cavity; Articular cartilage"]]},{"id":10502,"t":"Bone Development","s":"An X-ray of a 30-year-old shows the thin line labelled <b>B</b>. What does it represent?","o":["The fused (ossified) remnant of the growth plate, so growth in length has finished","An active growth plate, so the bone is still lengthening","A healed transverse fracture","The nutrient artery entering the shaft","The boundary of the medullary cavity"],"w":["Growth plates fuse in the late teens to early twenties.","A fracture line would not run neatly across the epiphysis.","The nutrient artery enters the diaphysis.","The medullary cavity lies in the shaft (D)."],"e":"B is the epiphyseal line: the ossified remnant of the epiphyseal (growth) plate, which fuses in the late teens–early twenties.","k":"Epiphyseal line = fused plate.","img":{"src":"10/o_longbone","cap":"Adult long bone. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What does the growth plate allow?","What does the growth plate allow?","Bone to keep increasing in length"],["","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","Thin dark line"],["","What is the thin line of remaining hyaline cartilage called?","What is the thin line of remaining hyaline cartilage called?","Growth plate"]]},{"id":10503,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Name the lattice-like bone labelled <b>C</b>, filling the epiphysis.","a":["spongy bone","cancellous bone","trabecular bone","spongy","cancellous"],"e":"C is spongy (cancellous, trabecular) bone; the shaft wall is compact bone.","k":"C = spongy bone.","img":{"src":"10/o_longbone","cap":"Adult long bone. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","Cortical (compact); Trabecular (spongy / cancellous)"],["","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","Spongy cancellous bone"]]},{"id":10504,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Name the central space labelled <b>D</b>, containing marrow in the shaft.","a":["medullary cavity","marrow cavity","medullary canal"],"e":"D is the medullary cavity, supplied by the nutrient artery.","k":"D = medullary cavity.","img":{"src":"10/o_longbone","cap":"Adult long bone. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What does the nutrient artery supply?","What does the nutrient artery supply?","The medullary cavity (marrow)"],["","What is the medullary cavity?","What is the medullary cavity?","Hollow centre of the shaft; Contains bone marrow"]]},{"id":10505,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Name the outer fibrous membrane labelled <b>E</b>, covering the shaft.","a":["periosteum"],"e":"E is the periosteum; the endosteum lines the medullary cavity.","k":"E = periosteum.","img":{"src":"10/o_longbone","cap":"Adult long bone. Image: public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum: covers the outer compact bone; Endosteum: lines the medullary cavity"]]},{"id":10521,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Name the cylindrical unit outlined by the black ellipse (pointed to by <b>C</b>), the structural unit of compact bone.","a":["osteon","haversian system","osteons"],"e":"C is an osteon (Haversian system): concentric lamellae (B) around a central Haversian canal (A), with osteocytes between the lamellae linked by canaliculi.","k":"C = osteon.","img":{"src":"10/o_osteon","cap":"Compact and spongy bone. Image: OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Osteon","Osteon","Found in cortical bone; Concentric lamellae around a central canal"]]},{"id":10522,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Name the channel <b>A</b> at the centre of the unit, carrying blood vessels and nerves.","a":["haversian canal","central canal","haversian","central (haversian) canal"],"e":"A is the Haversian (central) canal.","k":"A = Haversian canal.","img":{"src":"10/o_osteon","cap":"Compact and spongy bone. Image: OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What canal is at the centre of the osteocyte rings?","What canal is at the centre of the osteocyte rings?","Haversian canal"]]},{"id":10523,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"What are the rings labelled <b>B</b>?","o":["Concentric lamellae of bone matrix, with osteocytes between them","Circumferential lamellae of the outer periosteum","Trabeculae of spongy bone","Hyaline cartilage of the epiphyseal growth plate in a growing child","Layers of osteoclasts resorbing bone"],"w":["Those wrap the outside of the whole bone, not one canal.","Trabeculae are labelled D.","Cartilage is not organised into osteons.","Osteoclasts are single large resorbing cells, not rings."],"e":"B = concentric lamellae around the Haversian canal; osteocytes sit between them and connect through canaliculi.","k":"B = concentric lamellae.","img":{"src":"10/o_osteon","cap":"Compact and spongy bone. Image: OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Osteon","Osteon","Found in cortical bone; Concentric lamellae around a central canal"]]},{"id":10524,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Region <b>D</b> lacks osteons and is made of a lattice of thin struts. What is it?","o":["Spongy (cancellous) bone made of trabeculae","Compact (cortical) bone","Periosteum","Articular hyaline cartilage","Medullary cavity filled with yellow marrow"],"w":["Compact bone is the osteon-filled block on the left.","Periosteum is the outer membrane (E).","Cartilage has no trabeculae.","The cavity lies between the struts, not the struts themselves."],"e":"D is spongy (cancellous) bone: a lattice of trabeculae. The solid block packed with osteons is compact bone, covered externally by periosteum (E).","k":"D = spongy bone.","img":{"src":"10/o_osteon","cap":"Compact and spongy bone. Image: OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","Cortical (compact); Trabecular (spongy / cancellous)"],["","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","Spongy cancellous bone"]]},{"id":10001,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which list gives the five functions of the skeletal system covered?","o":["Support, protection, movement, blood formation, mineral storage","Support, protection, movement, blood formation, fat insulation","Support, protection, movement, hormone secretion, mineral storage","Support, protection, sensation, blood formation, mineral storage","Support, protection, movement, blood filtration, vitamin production"],"e":"The five functions: <b>support</b> (of muscles, ligaments, neurovasculature), <b>protection</b> (thoracic cage for heart and lungs; cranium for the brain), <b>movement</b> (bones as levers across joints), <b>blood formation</b> (haematopoiesis in marrow) and <b>mineral storage</b> (calcium and phosphate). Memory aid: Some People Move Bones Mightily.","w":["Fat storage (yellow marrow) is energy, not one of the five listed functions.","Hormones were not a listed function.","Sensation is not a skeletal function.","Blood formation, not filtration."],"k":"Skeleton: support, protection, movement, blood formation, mineral storage.","rn":[["","What are the 5 functions of the skeletal system?","What are the 5 functions of the skeletal system?","Support; Protection; Movement; Blood formation; Mineral storage; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10002,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Why is mineral storage important for the skeleton itself?","o":["Calcium and phosphate are vital for remodelling","Stored calcium lets bone conduct nerve impulses","Stored phosphate lets red marrow make blood cells","Stored calcium makes cartilage vascular so that it can heal","Stored minerals matter mainly for teeth, not bone"],"e":"Bone stores <b>calcium and phosphate</b>, which are vital for <b>bone maintenance and remodelling</b>. Osteoclasts free calcium to the blood, and osteoblasts lay down new matrix – the “bank” in the guide's analogy.","w":["Not stated.","Marrow makes blood independently.","Cartilage is avascular.","For bone maintenance and remodelling."],"k":"Bone minerals support remodelling.","rn":[["","Why does bone store calcium and phosphate?","Why does bone store calcium and phosphate?","For bone maintenance and remodelling"],["","Which 2 minerals does bone store?","Which 2 minerals does bone store?","Calcium; Phosphate"]]},{"id":10003,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","f":"sk7","s":"In a knee specimen, a ligament crosses the joint from anterior to posterior between the femur and tibia. Which skeletal component does it represent?","o":["Dense connective tissue (ACL)","Hyaline cartilage (articular)","Fibrocartilage (meniscus)","Neurovasculature (popliteal)","Spongy bone (epiphysis)"],"e":"The skeletal system has five components: <b>marrow, bone, cartilage, dense connective tissue and neurovasculature</b>. The <b>anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)</b> crossing the knee was the example of <b>dense connective tissue</b>.","w":["Ligaments are dense connective tissue.","The meniscus was not the structure described.","A ligament is not neurovasculature.","A ligament."],"k":"ACL = dense connective tissue.","rn":[["","Example of dense connective tissue in the skeleton?","Example of dense connective tissue in the skeleton?","Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)"],["","What are the 5 components of the skeletal system?","What are the 5 components of the skeletal system?","Marrow; Bone (osseous tissue); Cartilage; Dense connective tissue; Neurovasculature"]]},{"id":10004,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"The brownish tissue joining the ribs to the sternum in a thoracic cage specimen is which tissue?","o":["Costal (hyaline) cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage","Compact bone","Dense connective tissue ligament"],"e":"The brownish tissue connecting ribs to the sternum is <b>cartilage</b> – <b>costal cartilage</b>, a type of <b>hyaline</b> cartilage.","w":["Intervertebral discs and TMJ.","Epiglottis and auditory tube.","The ribs themselves.","Ligaments such as the ACL."],"k":"Costal cartilage is hyaline.","rn":[["","What connects the ribs to the sternum?","What connects the ribs to the sternum?","Costal cartilage"]]},{"id":10005,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Name the region labelled <b>B</b>, which connects the epiphysis to the shaft and is where the growth plate lay in childhood.","e":"B is the metaphysis, between the epiphysis and the diaphysis. Long bones grow in length at the epiphyseal (growth) plates, which fuse (ossify) in the late teens–early twenties.","k":"B = metaphysis.","img":{"src":"10/longbone","cap":"Adult long bone (humerus). Labels removed."},"a":["metaphysis","proximal metaphysis"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of a developing long bone?","What are the 3 parts of a developing long bone?","Epiphysis; Metaphysis; Diaphysis"],["","Where does the growth plate sit?","Where does the growth plate sit?","Between epiphysis and metaphysis"],["","What is the metaphysis?","What is the metaphysis?","Region joining the epiphysis to the diaphysis; Mostly trabecular bone"]]},{"id":10006,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Across the shaft of a long bone, from outside to inside, which order is correct?","o":["Periosteum → compact bone → endosteum → medullary cavity","Endosteum → compact bone → periosteum → medullary cavity","Periosteum → endosteum → compact bone → medullary cavity","Periosteum → spongy bone → endosteum → medullary cavity","Compact bone → periosteum → endosteum → medullary cavity"],"e":"From outside in: <b>periosteum</b> (outer connective tissue; “peri” = around) → <b>compact bone</b> → <b>endosteum</b> (inner connective-tissue lining) → hollow <b>medullary cavity</b> (marrow).","w":["Periosteum is outside.","Compact bone lies between them.","The shaft is compact bone.","Periosteum is outermost."],"k":"Periosteum → compact bone → endosteum → cavity.","rn":[["","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum: covers the outer compact bone; Endosteum: lines the medullary cavity"],["","What 3 layers surround the medullary cavity, outside to inside?","What 3 layers surround the medullary cavity, outside to inside?","Periosteum; Compact bone; Endosteum"]]},{"id":10007,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","f":"sk8","s":"A long bone is cut once through the diaphysis and once through the metaphysis. What would each cross-section show?","o":["Diaphysis: compact ring around a cavity; metaphysis: mostly spongy bone","Diaphysis: mostly spongy bone; metaphysis: compact ring around a cavity","Diaphysis: solid compact bone; metaphysis: solid compact bone","Diaphysis: compact ring around a cavity; metaphysis: hyaline cartilage","Diaphysis: growth plate; metaphysis: compact ring around a cavity"],"e":"A section through the <b>diaphysis</b> shows a <b>ring of compact (cortical) bone</b> around a <b>hollow medullary cavity</b>. A section through the <b>epiphysis/metaphysis</b> shows <b>spongy (trabecular)</b> bone.","w":["Swapped.","The diaphysis is hollow.","The metaphysis is mostly spongy bone.","Growth plate is at the ends."],"k":"Shaft = compact ring; ends = spongy bone.","rn":[["","What is the diaphysis?","What is the diaphysis?","Shaft of the bone; Ring of cortical bone around the medullary cavity"],["","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","A cross-section through the metaphysis is mostly what bone?","Spongy cancellous bone"]]},{"id":10008,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts compact and spongy bone?","o":["Compact: dense, strong; spongy: light lattice holding marrow","Compact: a trabecular lattice holding marrow; spongy: dense and strong","Compact: dense, holds marrow; spongy: lattice, gives strength","Compact: inner layer of all bones; spongy: outer layer","Compact and spongy are the same tissue in different places"],"e":"<b>Compact (cortical)</b>: dense outer layer, <b>strength and protection</b>, surrounds the shaft and outer surface of all bones. <b>Spongy (trabecular/cancellous)</b>: porous <b>lattice of trabeculae</b>, <b>reduces weight</b>, contains <b>spaces for marrow</b>; at the ends of long bones and inside flat bones.","w":["Swapped.","Spongy holds marrow.","Compact is outer.","Different."],"k":"Compact = strength; spongy = light, holds marrow.","rn":[["","What does a cross-section of a rib show, outside to inside?","What does a cross-section of a rib show, outside to inside?","Compact bone; Bone marrow within spongy bone"],["","Cortical bone: structure and job?","Cortical bone: structure and job?","Dense, compact outer layer; Strength and protection"],["","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","What are the 2 types of bone tissue?","Cortical (compact); Trabecular (spongy / cancellous)"]]},{"id":10009,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","f":"sk9","s":"If you snapped open a rib, what would you find inside?","o":["Spongy bone with marrow, as ribs are flat bones","A hollow medullary cavity, as in long bones","Spongy bone with yellow marrow only, as ribs are appendicular","Hyaline cartilage, as ribs are costal cartilage","A growth plate between two compact layers"],"e":"Ribs are <b>flat bones</b>; there is no hollow medullary cavity. Inside is <b>spongy bone with marrow</b> (red marrow persists because ribs are <b>axial</b>). The slide shows a cross-section of the mid-body of rib 7.","w":["No cavity in flat bones.","Ribs are axial; red marrow persists.","Costal cartilages are at the front only.","Not stated."],"k":"Flat bones: spongy bone + marrow, no hollow cavity.","rn":[["","What does a cross-section of a rib show, outside to inside?","What does a cross-section of a rib show, outside to inside?","Compact bone; Bone marrow within spongy bone"],["","Where is spongy bone found?","Where is spongy bone found?","Ends of long bones; Inside flat bones"],["","Flat bone: examples?","Flat bone: examples?","Ribs; Scapula; Cranial bones"]]},{"id":10010,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Where is red marrow found in adults?","o":["Axial skeleton, pelvis and proximal humerus and femur","Axial skeleton, pelvis and distal humerus and femur","Throughout all bones, as in children","Skull and ribs only","Appendicular skeleton and proximal humerus only"],"e":"<b>Red (haematopoietic) marrow</b>: widespread in children; in adults restricted to the <b>axial skeleton, pelvis and proximal ends of the humerus and femur</b> (e.g. skull, ribs, sternum, pelvis). The rest is <b>yellow</b> (adipose, energy storage).","w":["Proximal ends.","Restricted in adults.","Also sternum, vertebrae, pelvis, proximal long bones.","Axial."],"k":"Adult red marrow: axial, pelvis, proximal humerus/femur.","rn":[["","Where is red marrow found in adults?","Where is red marrow found in adults?","Axial skeleton; Pelvis; Proximal ends of humerus and femur"]]},{"id":10011,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Why do children have more red marrow than adults?","o":["Growing bones need blood and vascularisation","Growing bones need larger fat stores for energy during growth","Red marrow converts to cartilage in adults","Children have no fat to form yellow marrow","Red marrow is needed mainly to heal fractures"],"e":"Children's skeletons are <b>still growing</b>; they need nutrition, blood and <b>vascularisation</b> so bones can <b>lengthen and increase in diameter</b>. In adults, skeletal maturation has occurred, so predominantly <b>yellow marrow</b> (energy storage).","w":["That would be yellow marrow.","Yellow marrow (fat).","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Growing skeleton → more red marrow.","rn":[["","Why do children have more red marrow?","Why do children have more red marrow?","Skeleton is still growing; Needs nutrition, blood and vascularisation"],["","Why do adults have mostly yellow marrow?","Why do adults have mostly yellow marrow?","Skeleton has stopped growing; Less blood supply and vascularisation needed"]]},{"id":10012,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"What is the composition and role of yellow marrow?","o":["Mainly adipose tissue, for energy storage","Mainly haematopoietic tissue, making blood cells","Mainly hyaline cartilage, for growth","Mainly dense connective tissue, for support","Mainly osteoclasts, for resorption"],"e":"<b>Yellow marrow</b> is mainly <b>adipose tissue</b> for <b>energy storage</b>. <b>Red marrow</b> is haematopoietic.","w":["Red marrow.","Not marrow.","Not marrow.","Not stated."],"k":"Yellow marrow = fat, energy store.","rn":[["","Yellow marrow: made of and job?","Yellow marrow: made of and job?","Mainly adipose tissue; Energy storage"]]},{"id":10013,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Why does damaged cartilage heal slowly compared with, for example, a stretched muscle?","o":["It is avascular, so gets little oxygen","It has a rich blood supply that causes swelling and delays repair","It has no cells to repair the damage","It is constantly resorbed by osteoclasts","It is made of compact bone, which heals slowly"],"e":"Cartilage is <b>avascular</b> (very little or no blood supply), so <b>little oxygen and nutrition</b> reach it, giving <b>poor wound-healing properties</b> – “the one thing you must know”.","w":["It is avascular.","It has chondrocytes; the issue is blood supply.","Osteoclasts resorb bone.","Not bone."],"k":"Cartilage is avascular → heals slowly.","rn":[["","Why does cartilage heal poorly?","Why does cartilage heal poorly?","Avascular; Little oxygen and nutrients reach it"]]},{"id":10014,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","f":"sk10","s":"Which pairing of cartilage type and example is correct?","o":["Elastic – epiglottis; fibrocartilage – discs; hyaline – articular","Elastic – discs; fibrocartilage – epiglottis; hyaline – articular","Elastic – epiglottis; fibrocartilage – articular; hyaline – discs","Elastic – articular; fibrocartilage – discs; hyaline – epiglottis","Elastic – epiglottis; fibrocartilage – costal; hyaline – TMJ"],"e":"<b>Hyaline</b> (most common, glassy): costal cartilages, larynx, articular cartilage, bone development. <b>Fibrocartilage</b> (strong, resists compression): intervertebral discs, TMJ. <b>Elastic</b> (flexible, elastin): epiglottis, auditory tube.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Swapped.","Costal is hyaline; TMJ is fibrocartilage."],"k":"Hyaline, fibro, elastic examples.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of cartilage?","What are the 3 types of cartilage?","Hyaline; Fibrocartilage; Elastic"],["","The epiglottis is which type of cartilage?","The epiglottis is which type of cartilage?","Elastic"]]},{"id":10015,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"The epiglottis must bend backwards (retroflex) to close off the airway during swallowing. Which cartilage type suits this, and why?","o":["Elastic cartilage, as elastin makes it flexible","Fibrocartilage, as it resists compression","Hyaline cartilage, as it is the most common type","Elastic cartilage, as it resists compression","Fibrocartilage, as elastin makes it flexible"],"e":"The <b>epiglottis</b> is <b>elastic</b> cartilage: <b>flexible</b>, containing <b>elastin fibres</b>, so it can bend back to close the windpipe so food goes down the oesophagus.","w":["Discs/TMJ.","Not flexible enough.","Flexibility.","Fibrocartilage lacks the elastin."],"k":"Epiglottis = elastic (bends).","rn":[["","Elastic cartilage: features and examples?","Elastic cartilage: features and examples?","Flexible, contains elastin fibres; Epiglottis, auditory tube"]]},{"id":10016,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"The temporomandibular joint opens, closes, protrudes, retracts and grinds. Which cartilage type is present and why?","o":["Fibrocartilage: withstands tension and compression","Elastic cartilage, which bends and recoils easily","Hyaline cartilage, which is glassy and smooth","Fibrocartilage, which is flexible because of its elastin fibres","Elastic cartilage, which withstands compression"],"e":"The <b>TMJ</b> is very mobile; <b>fibrocartilage</b> holds it together and withstands <b>tension and compression</b>.","w":["Epiglottis.","Not the TMJ.","Elastin is elastic cartilage.","Not elastic."],"k":"TMJ = fibrocartilage.","rn":[["","Why does the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) need fibrocartilage?","Why does the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) need fibrocartilage?","The jaw is very mobile (open, close, protrude, retract, side to side); Fibrocartilage withstands tension and compression"]]},{"id":10017,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which type of cartilage forms the rings of the trachea?","o":["Hyaline","Elastic","Fibrocartilage","None","Calcified"],"e":"The cartilage in the <b>trachea</b> is <b>hyaline</b> (student question). Hyaline is also found in costal cartilages, larynx, articular surfaces and growth plates.","w":["Epiglottis.","Discs/TMJ.","It is present.","Not stated."],"k":"Trachea = hyaline.","rn":[["","Hyaline cartilage: features and examples?","Hyaline cartilage: features and examples?","Most common, smooth, glassy; Costal cartilages, larynx, articular cartilage"],["","Where is hyaline cartilage found besides articular surfaces?","Where is hyaline cartilage found besides articular surfaces?","Bone development and formation"]]},{"id":10018,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which artery supplies the bone marrow in the medullary cavity of a long bone?","e":"The nutrient artery enters the shaft to supply the marrow; epiphyseal, metaphyseal and periosteal arteries supply the other regions.","k":"Nutrient artery.","a":["nutrient artery","nutrient"],"rn":[["","What does the nutrient artery supply?","What does the nutrient artery supply?","The medullary cavity (marrow)"]]},{"id":10019,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Why is a fracture painful?","o":["More sensory nerves surround the periosteum than the endosteum","More sensory nerves surround the endosteum than the periosteum","Cartilage around the fracture is richly innervated","Bone marrow contains most of the pain receptors","Osteoclasts release chemicals that cause pain"],"e":"Nerves accompany vessels; there are <b>more sensory nerves around the periosteum than the endosteum</b>, so the body can detect pain when bone is damaged.","w":["Reversed.","Cartilage is avascular.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Periosteum richly innervated → fracture pain.","rn":[["","Why does the periosteum have more sensory nerves?","Why does the periosteum have more sensory nerves?","To sense pain when bone is damaged"],["","Why does a fracture hurt?","Why does a fracture hurt?","The periosteum is rich in sensory nerves"]]},{"id":10020,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about the neurovasculature of bone is correct?","o":["Veins accompany arteries and nerves accompany vessels","Arteries accompany nerves, but veins run separately","Nerves run separately from blood vessels in bone","Only the epiphyses receive blood vessels","Veins drain cartilage but not bone"],"e":"<b>Veins accompany arteries</b> (e.g. metaphyseal artery and vein) and <b>nerves accompany blood vessels</b>. The diagram shows bone is <b>living</b> and needs arteries and nerves.","w":["Veins accompany arteries.","Nerves accompany vessels.","Metaphyseal/nutrient arteries too.","Not stated."],"k":"Bone is vascular and innervated.","rn":[["","How do veins and nerves run in bone?","How do veins and nerves run in bone?","Veins accompany arteries; Nerves accompany blood vessels"]]},{"id":10021,"t":"Bone Classification","f":"sk13","s":"Which bone belongs to the appendicular skeleton?","o":["Scapula","Sternum","Hyoid","Sacrum","Coccyx"],"e":"<b>Axial</b>: skull, vertebrae, ribs, sternum, hyoid, sacrum, coccyx (central axis). <b>Appendicular</b>: bones attached to the axial skeleton – clavicles, <b>scapulae</b>, limbs, pelvic girdle.","w":["Axial.","Axial.","Axial.","Axial."],"k":"Scapula = appendicular.","rn":[["","Flat bone: examples?","Flat bone: examples?","Ribs; Scapula; Cranial bones"],["","What 2 bones form the acromioclavicular joint?","What 2 bones form the acromioclavicular joint?","Clavicle; Acromion of scapula"]]},{"id":10022,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which statement about the pelvis is correct?","o":["Pelvic girdle is appendicular; sacrum and coccyx are axial","Pelvic girdle is axial; sacrum and coccyx are appendicular","Pelvic girdle, sacrum and coccyx are all axial","Pelvic girdle, sacrum and coccyx are all appendicular","Pelvic girdle and sacrum are appendicular; coccyx is axial"],"e":"The <b>pelvic girdle</b> is <b>appendicular</b>, but the <b>sacrum</b> and <b>coccyx</b> are <b>axial</b>. The sacrum articulates with both sides of the pelvis.","w":["Swapped.","Girdle is appendicular.","Sacrum is axial.","Sacrum is axial."],"k":"Pelvic girdle appendicular; sacrum/coccyx axial.","rn":[["","Which bones make up the appendicular skeleton?","Which bones make up the appendicular skeleton?","Clavicles; Scapulae; Upper and lower limbs; Pelvic girdle"],["","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Skull; Vertebrae (33); Ribs (12 pairs); Sternum; Hyoid; Sacrum; Coccyx"]]},{"id":10023,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"How many vertebrae are there in each region, and in total?","o":["7 C, 12 T, 5 L, 5 S, 4 Co = 33","8 C, 12 T, 5 L, 5 S, 1 Co = 31","7 C, 12 T, 5 L, 5 S, 1 Co = 30","7 C, 10 T, 5 L, 5 S, 4 Co = 31","7 C, 12 T, 5 L, 1 S, 1 Co = 26"],"e":"Vertebrae: <b>7 – 12 – 5 – 5 – 4 = 33</b>. (31 is the number of <b>spinal nerve pairs</b>, a common confusion with the nervous system lecture.)","w":["That is spinal nerves.","4 coccygeal.","12 thoracic.","Sacral and coccygeal are fused groups of 5 and 4."],"k":"Vertebrae 7-12-5-5-4 = 33.","rn":[["","How many vertebrae are there?","How many vertebrae are there?","33"]]},{"id":10024,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A skier falls with the foot fixed and the leg twisting. The X-ray shows the pattern labelled <b>D</b>. Name this fracture pattern.","e":"D is a spiral fracture: the fracture line winds around the shaft. Fractures are classified by the bone involved, the location and the pattern of fragments: transverse, oblique, butterfly fragment, spiral, comminuted (many pieces) and segmental (two breaks).","k":"D = spiral.","img":{"src":"10/fractures","cap":"Fracture patterns of a long bone. Labels removed."},"a":["spiral","spiral fracture"],"rn":[["","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","Transverse; Oblique; Butterfly fragment; Spiral; Comminuted; Segmental"]]},{"id":10025,"t":"Bone Classification","f":"sk14","s":"Which bone is correctly matched with its shape classification?","o":["Patella – sesamoid","Carpal bones – long","Phalanges – short","Vertebrae – flat","Scapula – irregular"],"e":"Shapes: <b>long</b> (length > width: femur, humerus, phalanges), <b>short</b> (cuboidal: carpals), <b>flat</b> (ribs, scapula, cranial bones), <b>sesamoid</b> (within a tendon: <b>patella</b>), <b>irregular</b> (vertebrae).","w":["Short.","Long.","Irregular.","Flat."],"k":"Patella = sesamoid.","rn":[["","Sesamoid bone: shape and example?","Sesamoid bone: shape and example?","Formed within a tendon; Patella"]]},{"id":10026,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"A student says “phalanges are short bones because they are small.” Why is this wrong?","o":["Shape, not size, defines class: phalanges are longer than wide","Size, not shape, defines class: phalanges are small, so short","Shape defines class: phalanges are cuboidal, so short","Phalanges are sesamoid bones within tendons","Phalanges are irregular bones of the hand"],"e":"Classification depends on <b>shape, not size</b>. Phalanges have <b>length greater than width</b> → <b>long</b> bones; carpals are roughly cuboidal → <b>short</b>. The hand contains both.","w":["Shape matters.","They are long.","Not in tendons.","Long."],"k":"Long bones need not be big.","rn":[["","Long bone: shape and examples?","Long bone: shape and examples?","Length greater than width; Femur, humerus, phalanges"]]},{"id":10027,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"What is a bone that forms within a tendon (e.g. the patella) called?","e":"Sesamoid bones form within tendons.","k":"Sesamoid.","a":["sesamoid","sesamoid bone"],"rn":[["","Sesamoid bone: shape and example?","Sesamoid bone: shape and example?","Formed within a tendon; Patella"]]},{"id":10028,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which is the key difference between a foramen and a fossa?","o":["Foramen: a real hole; fossa: a depression","Foramen: a depression; fossa: a real hole","Foramen: a ridge; fossa: a projection","Foramen: a joint surface; fossa: a hole","Foramen: a hole on the femur only; fossa: on the skull only"],"e":"A <b>foramen</b> is a rounded <b>opening</b> – a real hole you could pass a thread through (e.g. foramen magnum). A <b>fossa</b> is a <b>depression</b> – a scoop you could only rest a finger in. This is the most common mix-up.","w":["Swapped.","Neither.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Foramen = hole; fossa = dip.","rn":[["","Fossa vs foramen","Fossa vs foramen","Fossa: just a dip; Foramen: a hole that nerves and vessels pass through"]]},{"id":10029,"t":"Bone Classification","f":"sk15","s":"Which statement about trochanters and tubercles is correct?","o":["Trochanters: large, rough, femur only; tubercles: small, e.g. humerus","Trochanters: small, rounded, humerus; tubercles: large, femur only","Trochanters and tubercles are both depressions","Trochanters: large, on humerus and femur; tubercles: on skull","Trochanters are joint surfaces; tubercles are holes"],"e":"<b>Trochanter</b>: large roughened projection <b>only on the femur</b>. <b>Tubercle</b>: small rounded projection (e.g. greater/lesser tubercles of the humerus). The lower limb has larger muscles needing bigger attachment points.","w":["Swapped.","Projections.","Femur only.","Projections."],"k":"Trochanter (femur only) vs tubercle.","rn":[["","What is a trochanter?","What is a trochanter?","Large roughened projection; Only on the femur"]]},{"id":10030,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which pairing of term and meaning is correct?","o":["Notch – an incomplete opening","Crest – a rounded opening","Tuberosity – a smooth articular surface","Groove – a large projection","Facet – a long ridge"],"e":"<b>Notch</b> = incomplete opening (scapula). Crest = long ridge; tuberosity = roughened projection; groove = narrow linear depression; facet = small smooth articular surface.","w":["Crest = ridge.","Tuberosity = rough projection.","Groove = linear depression.","Facet = small articular surface."],"k":"Terminology meanings.","rn":[["","What is a notch?","What is a notch?","An incomplete opening"]]},{"id":10031,"t":"Bone Classification","f":"sk17","s":"What distinguishes a condyle from an epicondyle?","o":["Condyle: rounded articular prominence; epicondyle: projection above it","Condyle: projection above an articular surface; epicondyle: the surface","Condyle: a hole for vessels; epicondyle: a depression","Condyle and epicondyle are two names for the same feature","Condyle: proximal end of long bones; epicondyle: distal end"],"e":"<b>Condyle</b>: rounded prominence with <b>articular (hyaline) cartilage</b>, usually at distal ends. <b>Epicondyle</b>: projection <b>above/adjacent to a condyle</b> (e.g. humerus). The proximal equivalent of a condyle is called the head.","w":["Swapped.","Neither.","Different.","Head is proximal."],"k":"Condyle articular; epicondyle beside it.","rn":[["","What is an epicondyle?","What is an epicondyle?","Projection above or next to a condyle"],["","What is a condyle?","What is a condyle?","Rounded prominence with articular cartilage (e.g. distal femur)"]]},{"id":10032,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which family of terms describes joint surfaces?","o":["Head, condyle and facet","Tubercle, trochanter and spine","Fossa, groove and notch","Foramen and crest","Tuberosity and epicondyle"],"e":"The guide groups terms: <b>bumps</b> (tubercle, tuberosity, trochanter, spine, crest, epicondyle), <b>dips and holes</b> (fossa, groove, notch, foramen) and <b>joint surfaces</b> (<b>head</b> proximal, <b>condyle</b> usually distal, <b>facet</b> small and flat).","w":["Bumps.","Dips.","Hole and ridge.","Bumps."],"k":"Joint surfaces: head, condyle, facet.","rn":[["","Is the head of the femur technically a condyle?","Is the head of the femur technically a condyle?","Yes, it acts on an axis; It is simply called the head"]]},{"id":10033,"t":"Joint Classification","f":"sk18","s":"How are joints classified into solid and cavitated groups?","o":["Solid: fibrous and cartilaginous; cavitated: synovial","Solid: synovial; cavitated: fibrous and cartilaginous","Solid: fibrous; cavitated: cartilaginous and synovial","Solid: cartilaginous; cavitated: fibrous and synovial","Solid: ball and socket; cavitated: hinge"],"e":"<b>Solid</b> joints (no synovial cavity, little or no mobility): <b>fibrous</b> and <b>cartilaginous</b>. <b>Cavitated</b> joints: <b>synovial</b> (cavity + fluid, freely movable).","w":["Swapped.","Cartilaginous is solid.","Fibrous is solid.","Those are synovial types."],"k":"Solid: fibrous/cartilaginous; cavitated: synovial.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of solid joints?","What are the 2 types of solid joints?","Fibrous; Cartilaginous"],["","Solid vs cavitated joints: main difference","Solid vs cavitated joints: main difference","Solid: no synovial cavity; Cavitated: synovial cavity"]]},{"id":10034,"t":"Joint Classification","f":"sk19","s":"Which pairing of fibrous joint and example is correct?","o":["Gomphosis – tooth in its socket","Gomphosis – pubic symphysis","Suture – radius and ulna","Syndesmosis – sagittal suture","Suture – growth plate"],"e":"Fibrous joints: <b>suture</b> (cranial bones, e.g. sagittal suture), <b>syndesmosis</b> (ligament/interosseous membrane, e.g. radius and ulna), <b>gomphosis</b> (peg-in-socket, <b>tooth in alveolar socket</b>).","w":["Cartilaginous.","Syndesmosis.","Suture.","Synchondrosis."],"k":"Fibrous: suture, syndesmosis, gomphosis.","rn":[["","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture: fibrous tissue; Syndesmosis: ligament/interosseous membrane; Gomphosis: peg in socket"],["","Gomphosis: structure and example?","Gomphosis: structure and example?","Peg in socket; Tooth root in alveolar socket"],["","Image card: What type of joint holds these teeth in place?","What type of joint holds these teeth in place?","Gomphosis (peg in socket); Fibrous joint; Tooth in alveolar socket"]]},{"id":10035,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"The radius and ulna are joined along their length by an interosseous membrane. Name this fibrous joint type.","e":"Fibrous joints: suture, syndesmosis (interosseous membrane), gomphosis (tooth in socket).","k":"Syndesmosis.","a":["syndesmosis"],"rn":[["","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture: fibrous tissue; Syndesmosis: ligament/interosseous membrane; Gomphosis: peg in socket"],["","Image card: What joint does the arrow point to? What type of joint is it?","What joint does the arrow point to? What type of joint is it?","Radius and ulna (interosseous membrane); Fibrous joint (syndesmosis)"],["","What are the 3 types of fibrous joints?","What are the 3 types of fibrous joints?","Suture; Syndesmosis; Gomphosis"]]},{"id":10036,"t":"Joint Classification","f":"sk20","s":"What distinguishes a symphysis from a synchondrosis?","o":["Symphysis: fibrocartilage (pubic); synchondrosis: hyaline (growth plate)","Symphysis: hyaline (growth plate); synchondrosis: fibrocartilage (pubic)","Symphysis: fibrocartilage (growth plate); synchondrosis: hyaline (pubic)","Symphysis: ligament (radius–ulna); synchondrosis: hyaline (growth plate)","Symphysis: synovial cavity; synchondrosis: fibrocartilage"],"e":"Cartilaginous joints: <b>symphysis</b> – <b>fibrocartilage</b> (intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis); <b>synchondrosis</b> – <b>hyaline cartilage</b> (epiphyseal growth plate).","w":["Swapped.","Examples swapped.","Syndesmosis.","No cavity."],"k":"Symphysis = fibro; synchondrosis = hyaline.","rn":[["","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis: fibrocartilage; Synchondrosis: hyaline cartilage"],["","Image card: What type of joint does this histology show? What connects the bone?","What type of joint does this histology show? What connects the bone?","Synchondrosis (epiphyseal growth plate); Hyaline cartilage"]]},{"id":10037,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"The pubic symphysis can separate slightly during childbirth. Name its joint classification (cartilaginous …).","e":"Symphyses are cartilaginous joints with fibrocartilage (pubic symphysis, intervertebral discs); synchondroses use hyaline cartilage (growth plate).","k":"Symphysis.","a":["symphysis","secondary cartilaginous","cartilaginous symphysis"],"rn":[["","When can the pubic symphysis separate slightly?","When can the pubic symphysis separate slightly?","Childbirth"],["","Symphysis","Symphysis","Cartilaginous joint connected by fibrocartilage"]]},{"id":10038,"t":"Joint Classification","f":"sk21","s":"Which features define a synovial joint?","o":["Synovial cavity, membrane, fluid, articular cartilage; free","Synovial cavity, membrane and fibrocartilage; little movement","No cavity; bones joined by fibrous tissue; immovable","No cavity; bones joined by hyaline cartilage; slightly movable at most","Synovial fluid but no capsule; freely movable"],"e":"Synovial joints: a <b>synovial (joint) cavity</b>, <b>synovial membrane</b> lining the capsule, <b>articular cartilage</b> covering bone ends, and <b>synovial fluid</b> lubricating movement; <b>freely movable</b>.","w":["Articular cartilage; freely movable.","Fibrous.","That is a synchondrosis.","Capsule lined by membrane."],"k":"Synovial: cavity, membrane, cartilage, fluid.","rn":[["","What 3 things does a synovial joint have?","What 3 things does a synovial joint have?","Synovial membrane; Synovial cavity; Articular cartilage"]]},{"id":10039,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which synovial joint type is multiaxial, allowing flexion/extension, abduction/adduction and rotation (e.g. hip, shoulder)?","e":"Ball and socket joints are multiaxial.","k":"Ball and socket.","a":["ball and socket","ball-and-socket","ball and socket joint"],"rn":[["","Which synovial joint is multiaxial?","Which synovial joint is multiaxial?","Ball and socket"],["","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, rotation; Hip, shoulder"]]},{"id":10040,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"A patient can flex, extend, abduct and adduct their index finger at the knuckle but cannot rotate it. Which joint type is this?","o":["Condyloid (biaxial)","Saddle (biaxial)","Ball and socket (multiaxial)","Hinge (uniaxial)","Pivot (uniaxial)"],"e":"The <b>metacarpophalangeal</b> (knuckle) joint is <b>condyloid</b>: <b>biaxial</b> – flexion/extension, abduction/adduction and circumduction, but <b>no rotation</b> (the lecturer demonstrated by pointing a finger).","w":["Thumb CMC.","Rotation would be possible.","No abduction.","Rotation only."],"k":"Condyloid: biaxial, no rotation.","rn":[["","Which synovial joints are biaxial?","Which synovial joints are biaxial?","Condyloid; Saddle"],["","Condyloid joint: movements and example?","Condyloid joint: movements and example?","Flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, circumduction (no rotation); Metacarpophalangeal joint (knuckles)"]]},{"id":10041,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Turning the head to say “no” occurs at which pivot joint?","e":"The atlanto-axial joint (C1–C2) is a pivot joint allowing rotation.","k":"Atlanto-axial.","a":["atlanto-axial","atlantoaxial","atlanto axial","atlanto-axial joint","c1-c2","c1 c2"],"rn":[["","Pivot joint: movement and example?","Pivot joint: movement and example?","Rotation; Atlanto-axial joint (C1 and C2)"],["","Which joint lets you turn your head to say \"no\"?","Which joint lets you turn your head to say \"no\"?","Atlanto-axial (pivot) joint"],["","What 2 vertebrae form the atlanto-axial joint?","What 2 vertebrae form the atlanto-axial joint?","Atlas (C1); Axis (C2)"]]},{"id":10042,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which pairing of synovial joint type and example is correct?","o":["Saddle – thumb CMC","Saddle – knuckle (MCP) joint","Hinge – hip","Pivot – elbow","Plane – atlanto-axial"],"e":"Saddle (biaxial, two planes): <b>carpometacarpal joint of the thumb</b>. Hinge: elbow. Plane: acromioclavicular. Pivot: atlanto-axial. Condyloid: MCP.","w":["MCP is condyloid.","Ball and socket.","Hinge.","Pivot."],"k":"Saddle = thumb CMC.","rn":[["","Saddle joint: movement and example?","Saddle joint: movement and example?","Movement in two planes; Carpometacarpal joint of the thumb"]]},{"id":10043,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"When you shrug your shoulders, the acromioclavicular joint moves up and down in one plane. What type is it?","o":["Plane (gliding)","Hinge","Pivot","Saddle","Condyloid"],"e":"The <b>acromioclavicular</b> joint is a <b>plane</b> joint: gliding/sliding, usually uniaxial – it only moves up and down when you shrug (elevation/depression).","w":["Elbow.","Atlanto-axial.","Thumb.","MCP."],"k":"Acromioclavicular = plane.","rn":[["","Plane joint: movement and example?","Plane joint: movement and example?","Gliding / sliding; Acromioclavicular joint"],["","How does the acromioclavicular joint move when you shrug?","How does the acromioclavicular joint move when you shrug?","Only up and down (one plane)"]]},{"id":10044,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"What is the relationship between the number of axes and the joint type?","o":["Uniaxial: hinge, pivot; biaxial: condyloid, saddle; multiaxial: ball and socket","Uniaxial: hinge, saddle; biaxial: condyloid, pivot; multiaxial: ball and socket","Uniaxial: pivot, condyloid; biaxial: hinge, saddle; multiaxial: ball and socket","Uniaxial: hinge, pivot; biaxial: ball and socket; multiaxial: condyloid, saddle","Uniaxial: ball and socket; biaxial: condyloid, saddle; multiaxial: hinge, pivot"],"e":"Number of axes = number of planes: <b>uniaxial</b> (hinge, pivot; plane usually), <b>biaxial</b> (condyloid, saddle), <b>multiaxial</b> (ball and socket).","w":["Saddle biaxial; pivot uniaxial.","Condyloid biaxial; hinge uniaxial.","Ball and socket multiaxial.","Reversed."],"k":"Axes by joint type.","rn":[["","What are the 6 types of synovial joint?","What are the 6 types of synovial joint?","Ball and socket; Condyloid; Saddle; Hinge; Pivot; Plane; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10045,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"A bicep curl bends the elbow. Which movement is this, and at which joint type?","o":["Flexion at a hinge joint","Extension at a hinge joint","Rotation at a pivot joint","Abduction at a ball and socket joint","Circumduction at a plane joint"],"e":"A bicep curl is <b>flexion</b> (decreasing the angle) at the <b>elbow</b>, a <b>hinge</b> joint (flexion and extension only).","w":["Straightening.","Turning the head.","Not the elbow.","Not the elbow."],"k":"Elbow flexion = hinge.","rn":[["","Hinge joint: movement and example?","Hinge joint: movement and example?","Flexion and extension only; Elbow"]]},{"id":10046,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"What is circumduction?","o":["Circular: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction","Turning of a limb around its own long axis","A circular movement combining rotation and gliding only","Movement of a limb towards the midline","Bending that reduces the angle at a joint"],"e":"<b>Circumduction</b>: circular movement combining flexion, extension, abduction and adduction (e.g. drawing a circle with the arm). Rotation is turning around the long axis.","w":["Rotation.","Not the definition.","Adduction.","Flexion."],"k":"Circumduction = combined circular movement.","rn":[["","What is circumduction?","What is circumduction?","Circular movement combining flexion, extension, abduction and adduction"]]},{"id":10047,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk24","s":"About how many bones are present at birth and in adults, and why does the number fall?","o":["~300 at birth, ~206 in adults, as bones fuse","~206 at birth, ~300 in adults, as bones split","~300 at birth, ~206 in adults, as bones are resorbed","~206 at birth and in adults","~33 at birth, ~206 in adults, as bones form"],"e":"About <b>300 bones at birth → about 206 in adults</b>, as bones <b>fuse (ossify)</b>. Example: the os coxae starts as ilium, ischium and pubis and fuses into one.","w":["Fall with age.","Fusion, not resorption.","Number falls.","Not the figures."],"k":"~300 → ~206 bones by fusion.","rn":[["","How many bones in the embryo vs adult?","How many bones in the embryo vs adult?","About 300 in the embryo; About 206 in the adult"]]},{"id":10048,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk24","s":"An X-ray of a 3-year-old's ankle shows separate bony sections. What is the correct interpretation?","o":["Normal ossification centres","Multiple fractures from a fall","Sesamoid bones within tendons","A malunited old fracture","Osteoporosis with fragmentation"],"e":"Separate pieces in children are <b>normal ossification centres</b>, <b>not fractures</b>; in adults they become the tibia and fibula.","w":["Normal.","Not sesamoid.","Normal.","Normal."],"k":"Child X-ray: ossification centres ≠ fractures.","rn":[["","How does intramembranous ossification spread?","How does intramembranous ossification spread?","From centres"],["","Where are the secondary ossification centres?","Where are the secondary ossification centres?","Epiphyses"],["","What do the different ossification centres encourage?","What do the different ossification centres encourage?","Bone growth lengthwise"]]},{"id":10049,"t":"Bone Development","s":"When does bone development begin, and when is skeletal maturity reached?","o":["Weeks 6–7 of embryonic life; maturity at ~20–25","Weeks 6–7 of embryonic life; maturity at ~16–18","At birth; maturity at ~20–25","Weeks 1–2 of embryonic life; maturity at ~30","At puberty; maturity at ~18"],"e":"Bone development begins at about <b>weeks 6–7</b> (embryonic); skeletal maturity at about <b>20–25 years</b> – so most students still have immature skeletons.","w":["20–25.","Weeks 6–7.","Weeks 6–7.","Weeks 6–7."],"k":"Ossification starts wk 6–7; maturity 20–25 y."},{"id":10050,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk25","s":"Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","o":["Flat skull bones, mandible and clavicle","Flat skull bones, mandible and vertebrae","Long bones, vertebrae and pelvis","Skull base, mandible and clavicle","Flat skull bones, pelvis and femur"],"e":"<b>Intramembranous</b> (“within membrane”): <b>flat skull bones (cranial vault), mandible, clavicle</b>. <b>Endochondral</b>: most of the body – long bones, vertebrae, pelvis, skull base.","w":["Vertebrae are endochondral.","Endochondral.","Skull base is endochondral.","Endochondral."],"k":"Intramembranous: skull vault, mandible, clavicle.","rn":[["","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Cranial vault (flat skull bones); Mandible (maxilla/mandible); Clavicle"]]},{"id":10051,"t":"Bone Development","s":"What is the starting tissue in each type of ossification?","o":["Endochondral: hyaline cartilage template; intramembranous: mesenchyme","Endochondral: mesenchyme; intramembranous: hyaline cartilage template","Endochondral: fibrocartilage template; intramembranous: mesenchyme","Endochondral: elastic cartilage template; intramembranous: mesenchyme","Both start from a hyaline cartilage template"],"e":"<b>Endochondral</b> (“within cartilage”) starts from a <b>hyaline cartilage template</b>. <b>Intramembranous</b> starts from <b>mesenchyme</b> – stem cells → osteoblasts → osteoid → woven bone, with no cartilage.","w":["Swapped.","Hyaline.","Hyaline.","Intramembranous has no cartilage."],"k":"Endochondral: hyaline template; intramembranous: mesenchyme.","rn":[["","Intramembranous vs endochondral: starting material","Intramembranous vs endochondral: starting material","Intramembranous: mesenchymal stem cells; Endochondral: hyaline cartilage model"]]},{"id":10052,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk26","s":"Put the steps of endochondral ossification in order.","o":["Template → calcification → primary centre → secondary centres → plate fuses","Template → primary centre → calcification → secondary centres → plate fuses","Template → calcification → secondary centres → primary centre → plate fuses","Calcification → template → primary centre → secondary centres → plate fuses","Mesenchyme → osteoid → woven bone → plate fuses"],"e":"Steps: <b>hyaline cartilage template</b> → <b>calcification</b> (weeks 6–8) → vessels invade forming the <b>primary centre</b> in the diaphysis → <b>secondary centres</b> in the epiphyses → a <b>growth plate</b> remains between epiphysis and metaphysis → it narrows until <b>epiphyseal fusion</b> (~20–25 y).","w":["Calcification first.","Primary first (diaphysis).","Template first.","Intramembranous."],"k":"Endochondral sequence.","rn":[["","What are the 5 stages of endochondral ossification?","What are the 5 stages of endochondral ossification?","Hyaline cartilaginous template; Template replaced by bone; Different ossification centres emerge; Growth plate between epiphysis and metaphysis; Growth plate narrows, replaced by bone"],["","Where are the secondary ossification centres?","Where are the secondary ossification centres?","Epiphyses"],["","Where is the primary ossification centre?","Where is the primary ossification centre?","Diaphysis"]]},{"id":10053,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Why do blood vessels need to invade the calcified cartilage template?","o":["Living bone needs oxygen and nutrients; vessels make centres","Vessels remove calcium so that the template remains as cartilage","Vessels make the cartilage avascular","Vessels stop bone growth at the ends","Vessels form the periosteum only"],"e":"Bone must be <b>living</b>, needing oxygen, nutrients and blood drainage. Blood vessels grow in and create <b>primary ossification sites</b>; secondary centres form at the ends because they need a blood supply too.","w":["The opposite.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Vascular invasion creates ossification centres.","rn":[["","Why does bone need blood vessels to ossify?","Why does bone need blood vessels to ossify?","Oxygen and nutrients; Drainage to make bone living"]]},{"id":10054,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk28","s":"On a knee X-ray of a teenager, a thin dark line crosses the end of the femur. What is it?","o":["The growth plate – cartilage not seen on X-ray","A fracture through the end of the femur","The medullary cavity seen end-on","A nutrient foramen for the femoral artery","An area of osteoporotic bone"],"e":"On X-ray cartilage does not show up, so the <b>growth plate</b> appears as a <b>thin dark line</b> across the end of the bone. It is <b>not a fracture</b>.","w":["Not a fracture.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Growth plate = dark line on X-ray.","rn":[["","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","Thin dark line"]]},{"id":10055,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Where do long bones grow in length, and what stops this growth?","o":["At the epiphyseal plates; stops when they fuse","At the diaphysis; stops when the medullary cavity fuses","At the epiphyseal plates; stops at puberty when the plates first open","At the periosteum; stops when the periosteum thins","At the articular cartilage; stops at about 40"],"e":"Long bones grow in <b>length at the epiphyseal plates</b>, where epiphyseal cartilage converts to bone. In the <b>late teens to early twenties</b> the plates <b>fuse (ossify)</b> – <b>epiphyseal fusion</b> – and lengthwise growth stops.","w":["Plates.","They fuse in late teens/twenties.","Width, not length.","Not stated."],"k":"Growth at physis until fusion.","rn":[["","Epiphyseal fusion","Epiphyseal fusion","Growth plates fuse (ossify); Epiphyses fuse with the diaphysis"]]},{"id":10056,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which joint classification describes the growth plate?","o":["Synchondrosis (hyaline)","Symphysis (fibrocartilage)","Syndesmosis (ligament)","Suture (fibrous)","Synovial (cavity)"],"e":"The growth plate is the <b>synchondrosis</b> from the joints section: two bones meeting with a <b>hyaline cartilage</b> framework.","w":["Pubic symphysis, discs.","Radius–ulna.","Cranial.","Not a cavity."],"k":"Growth plate = synchondrosis.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of joint does this histology show? What connects the bone?","What type of joint does this histology show? What connects the bone?","Synchondrosis (epiphyseal growth plate); Hyaline cartilage"],["","Synchondrosis","Synchondrosis","Cartilaginous joint connected by hyaline cartilage"],["","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis: fibrocartilage; Synchondrosis: hyaline cartilage"]]},{"id":10057,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Name the fracture pattern labelled <b>E</b>, in which the bone is broken into many pieces.","e":"E is comminuted: the bone is broken into many fragments.","k":"E = comminuted.","img":{"src":"10/fractures","cap":"Fracture patterns of a long bone. Labels removed."},"a":["comminuted","comminuted fracture"],"rn":[["","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","Transverse; Oblique; Butterfly fragment; Spiral; Comminuted; Segmental"]]},{"id":10058,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Articular cartilage is typically located on which part of a long bone?","o":["Epiphysis","Diaphysis","Metaphysis","Medullary cavity","Periosteum"],"e":"Quick quiz answer: <b>B, the epiphysis</b> – the articulating ends of the bone.","w":["Shaft.","Linking region.","Hollow centre.","Outer covering."],"k":"Articular cartilage on the epiphysis.","rn":[["","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Epiphysis"]]},{"id":10059,"t":"Bone Development","f":"sk29","s":"Which statement about intramembranous ossification is correct?","o":["Mesenchyme → osteoblasts → osteoid → woven bone","Mesenchyme → chondrocytes → cartilage → woven bone","Hyaline cartilage template → osteoblasts → osteoid → woven bone","Mesenchyme → osteoclasts → osteoid → lamellar bone","It forms long bones via a growth plate"],"e":"Intramembranous: <b>mesenchymal cells → osteoblasts → osteoid → woven bone</b>, no cartilage. It starts differently from endochondral but ends with bone. More detail in Year 2.","w":["No cartilage.","No cartilage stage.","Osteoblasts lay osteoid.","Flat bones."],"k":"Intramembranous: no cartilage stage.","rn":[["","What are the 4 stages of intramembranous ossification?","What are the 4 stages of intramembranous ossification?","Mesenchymal (stem) cells; Osteoblasts; Osteoid (bone matrix); Developing woven bone"],["","Osteoid","Osteoid","Bone matrix secreted by osteoblasts; Becomes woven bone"]]},{"id":10060,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Fracture <b>F</b> has two separate fracture lines isolating a section of shaft. Which statement is correct?","o":["F is segmental; A is transverse","F is comminuted; A is oblique","F is spiral; A is transverse","F is segmental; A is oblique","F is a butterfly fragment; A is transverse"],"e":"Segmental fractures have two breaks isolating a segment (F). A transverse fracture runs straight across the shaft (A); an oblique fracture runs at an angle (B).","w":["Comminuted means many pieces, not two separate breaks; A runs straight across.","A spiral winds around the shaft (D).","A runs straight across the shaft: transverse.","A butterfly fragment is a wedge-shaped piece (C)."],"k":"Two breaks = segmental.","img":{"src":"10/fractures","cap":"Fracture patterns of a long bone. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","Transverse; Oblique; Butterfly fragment; Spiral; Comminuted; Segmental"]]},{"id":10061,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Which pairing of bone cell and role is correct?","o":["Osteoblast builds; osteoclast resorbs; osteocyte regulates","Osteoclast builds; osteoblast resorbs; osteocyte regulates","Osteoblast builds; osteocyte resorbs; osteoclast regulates","Osteocyte builds; osteoclast resorbs; osteoblast regulates","Osteoblast resorbs; osteoclast builds; osteocyte regulates"],"e":"<b>Osteoblasts build</b> (deposit collagen and bone matrix – osteoid), <b>osteocytes</b> are mature bone cells that <b>regulate</b> growth, loss and maintenance (via canaliculi), <b>osteoclasts resorb</b> (acid and enzymes; multinucleated; resorption bays).","w":["Swapped.","Osteoclast resorbs.","Osteoblast builds.","Swapped."],"k":"Blasts build, cytes regulate, clasts resorb.","rn":[["","Osteoblasts vs osteocytes vs osteoclasts: jobs","Osteoblasts vs osteocytes vs osteoclasts: jobs","Osteoblasts build; Osteocytes regulate; Osteoclasts resorb; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10062,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"An osteoblast becomes fully surrounded by the matrix it secreted. What does it become?","e":"Trapped osteoblasts become osteocytes in lacunae, communicating via canaliculi.","k":"Osteocyte.","a":["osteocyte","osteocytes"],"rn":[["","What canal is at the centre of the osteocyte rings?","What canal is at the centre of the osteocyte rings?","Haversian canal"]]},{"id":10063,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Cell <b>X</b> is large and multinucleated and sits in a scooped-out bay on the bone surface. What is it and how does it act?","o":["Osteoclast: acid and enzymes break down matrix, freeing calcium","Osteoblast: lays down osteoid that later mineralises","Osteocyte: senses load via canaliculi in a lacuna","Osteoclast: lays down new lamellar bone","Chondroblast: forms the soft callus"],"e":"Osteoclasts sit in resorption bays and use acid and enzymes to break down bone matrix, resorbing bone for remodelling and freeing calcium into the blood stream.","w":["Osteoblasts build bone; they are not in resorption bays.","Osteocytes sit inside the bone in lacunae.","Osteoclasts resorb; osteoblasts form bone.","Chondroblasts form cartilage."],"k":"Multinucleate cell in a bay = osteoclast.","img":{"src":"10/osteoclast","cap":"Bone surface, H&amp;E. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What do osteoclasts use to break down bone matrix?","What do osteoclasts use to break down bone matrix?","Acid; Enzymes"]]},{"id":10064,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","f":"sk34","s":"How do osteocytes communicate with each other?","o":["Via canaliculi","Through the Haversian canal only","Through synovial fluid in joints","Through the bloodstream only","Through the growth plate"],"e":"Osteocytes sit in rings around the <b>Haversian (central) canal</b>; their processes run through <b>canaliculi</b> (“little canals”), signalling e.g. that more bone is needed where compression or tension increases.","w":["Canaliculi connect them.","Not stated.","Not only blood.","Not stated."],"k":"Canaliculi link osteocytes.","rn":[["","Canaliculi","Canaliculi","Little spindles connecting osteocytes; Let them communicate"],["","Image card: What are these cells with long branching processes?","What are these cells with long branching processes?","Osteocytes (mature bone cells); Connected by canaliculi"]]},{"id":10065,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"What is bone remodelling, and what does it achieve?","o":["Osteoclasts resorb old bone, osteoblasts replace it; maintains and protects","Osteoblasts resorb old bone, osteoclasts replace it; maintains and protects","Osteoclasts resorb old bone and nothing replaces it","Formation of new bone during childhood only","Fusion of the growth plates in late adolescence"],"e":"<b>Remodelling</b> = removal and resorption of old or damaged bone by <b>osteoclasts</b> and replacement by <b>osteoblasts</b>. It maintains <b>mechanical support</b>, <b>protects vital organs</b> and controls <b>mineral homeostasis</b>, continuing after age 25.","w":["Swapped.","Replacement occurs.","Lifelong.","Not remodelling."],"k":"Remodelling: resorb then replace.","rn":[["","In remodelling, which cell acts first at a site?","In remodelling, which cell acts first at a site?","Osteoclast resorbs, then osteoblast forms new bone"],["","Bone remodelling (maintenance)","Bone remodelling (maintenance)","Old or damaged bone removed by osteoclasts; Replaced with new bone by osteoblasts"]]},{"id":10066,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"What is the difference between remodelling and modelling?","o":["Remodelling: same site, replaces bone; modelling: different sites, changes shape","Remodelling: different sites, changes shape; modelling: same site, replaces bone","Remodelling and modelling are the same process","Remodelling: childhood only; modelling: adulthood only","Remodelling: fracture healing only; modelling: growth only"],"e":"<b>Remodelling</b>: resorption <b>followed by</b> formation at the <b>same location</b> – replacement/maintenance (“redecorating the same room”). <b>Modelling</b>: formation and resorption <b>independently at different locations</b> – changes in size, shape and morphology (“building an extension”).","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Both lifelong.","Not stated."],"k":"Remodelling replaces; modelling reshapes.","rn":[["","Modelling","Modelling","Bone formation and resorption independently at different locations; Changes size, shape, morphology"]]},{"id":10067,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","f":"sk36","s":"A healed fracture is angled backwards. Over time bone is resorbed posteriorly and laid down anteriorly, straightening it. Which process and law apply?","o":["Modelling, according to Wolff’s Law","Remodelling, according to Wolff’s Law","Modelling, by endochondral ossification","Remodelling at the same location","Epiphyseal fusion"],"e":"Formation and resorption at <b>different locations</b> to change shape = <b>modelling</b>. <b>Wolff's Law</b>: bone <b>adapts to the mechanical loads</b> placed on it – formed where compressed, resorbed where under tension.","w":["Different sites.","Not ossification.","Different sites.","Not stated."],"k":"Reshaping by load = modelling (Wolff's Law).","rn":[["","Wolff's law","Wolff's law","Bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it"],["","Example of Wolff's law?","Example of Wolff's law?","Bent broken bone; Resorbed on one side, laid down on the other"]]},{"id":10068,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Which law states that bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it?","e":"Wolff’s Law: bone adapts to the loads placed on it (e.g. exercised sheep tibiae were denser).","k":"Wolff’s Law.","a":["wolff's law","wolffs law","wolff law","wolff"],"rn":[["","Wolff's law","Wolff's law","Bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it"]]},{"id":10069,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","f":"sk37","s":"In sheep studies, how did exercised tibiae differ from controls?","o":["Denser, thicker compact bone","Thinner, more porous compact bone","More cartilage at the growth plate","Earlier growth plate fusion","No difference from controls"],"e":"Exercised tibiae had <b>denser compact bone</b> than controls – <b>Wolff's Law</b> in action; lack of exercise harms bone health.","w":["Opposite.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Denser."],"k":"Exercise → denser bone.","rn":[["","Image card: What do these two sheep tibia cross-sections show?","What do these two sheep tibia cross-sections show?","Exercise increases bone density; Exercised: denser compact bone"]]},{"id":10070,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"Which pairing of vitamin and bone role is correct?","o":["Vitamin D – calcium absorption (rickets)","Vitamin D – connective tissue (scurvy)","Vitamin C – calcium absorption (rickets)","Vitamin A – calcium absorption (rickets)","Vitamin C – bone remodelling (rickets)"],"e":"<b>Vitamin A</b>: bone remodelling. <b>Vitamin C</b>: connective tissue (deficiency → <b>scurvy</b>, poor wound healing). <b>Vitamin D</b>: <b>calcium absorption</b> (deficiency → <b>rickets</b> in children).","w":["Vitamin C.","Vitamin D.","Vitamin D.","Vitamin A is remodelling; C is connective tissue."],"k":"Vit A remodelling; C connective tissue; D calcium.","rn":[["","What is vitamin D needed for?","What is vitamin D needed for?","Calcium absorption"]]},{"id":10071,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"A child has soft, bowed legs due to poor calcium absorption. Name the condition.","e":"Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets in children (poor calcium absorption, soft bowed bones).","k":"Rickets.","a":["rickets"],"rn":[["","Image card: What condition is shown in this child's lower limbs?","What condition is shown in this child's lower limbs?","Rickets"]]},{"id":10072,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts osteoporosis and osteopetrosis?","o":["Porosis: resorption > formation; petrosis: less resorption, brittle","Osteoporosis: less resorption, dense bone; osteopetrosis: resorption exceeds formation, porous","Osteoporosis: formation > resorption, dense; osteopetrosis: resorption > formation","Osteoporosis and osteopetrosis are both caused by excess formation","Osteoporosis: too few osteoclasts; osteopetrosis: too many"],"e":"<b>Osteoporosis</b>: <b>resorption > formation</b> (osteoclasts outpace osteoblasts) → weak, porous bone that breaks easily. <b>Osteopetrosis</b>: <b>decreased resorption</b> (osteoclasts under-active) → dense but <b>brittle</b> bone.","w":["Swapped.","Wrong.","Wrong.","Reversed."],"k":"Osteoporosis vs osteopetrosis.","rn":[["","Osteopetrosis","Osteopetrosis","Decreased resorption; Dense, brittle bone"],["","Osteoporosis","Osteoporosis","Resorption > formation; Weak, porous bone"]]},{"id":10073,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Which hormonal changes affect bone health?","o":["Acromegaly (excess growth hormone) and menopause","Acromegaly (low growth hormone) and menopause","Diabetes and thyroid storm","Puberty and pregnancy","Menopause and vitamin C deficiency"],"e":"Hormonal changes: e.g. <b>acromegaly</b> (excess growth hormone) and <b>menopause</b>.","w":["Excess.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Vitamin, not hormone."],"k":"Acromegaly and menopause affect bone.","rn":[["","What can keep growth plates open, causing uncontrolled growth?","What can keep growth plates open, causing uncontrolled growth?","Excess growth hormone"],["","Acromegaly","Acromegaly","Uncontrollable growth, especially of long bones; Too much growth hormone"],["","Image card: What condition does the man on the left have? What causes it?","What condition does the man on the left have? What causes it?","Acromegaly; Too much growth hormone"]]},{"id":10074,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Name the fracture pattern labelled <b>A</b>, running straight across the shaft.","e":"A transverse fracture runs straight across the shaft; B (oblique) runs at an angle.","k":"A = transverse.","img":{"src":"10/fractures","cap":"Fracture patterns of a long bone. Labels removed."},"a":["transverse","transverse fracture"],"rn":[["","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","What are the 6 fracture patterns?","Transverse; Oblique; Butterfly fragment; Spiral; Comminuted; Segmental"]]},{"id":10075,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"When does a fracture occur, and how are fractures classified?","o":["Load exceeds strength; by bone, location and pattern","Load exceeds strength; by age and colour on X-ray","Cartilage calcifies; by bone, location and pattern","Infection weakens bone; by bone and location","Load exceeds strength; by healing time only"],"e":"A fracture happens when the <b>load exceeds bone strength</b>; fractures are classified by the <b>bone involved, location and pattern of fragments</b>. Detailed types are covered in Year 2 MSK.","w":["Not classification.","Load.","Load.","Not stated."],"k":"Fracture: load > strength.","rn":[["","When does a bone fracture?","When does a bone fracture?","Load exceeds bone strength"]]},{"id":10076,"t":"Bone Fractures","f":"sk39","s":"Put the four stages of fracture healing in order.","o":["Haematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodelling","Haematoma → hard callus → soft callus → remodelling","Soft callus → haematoma → hard callus → remodelling","Haematoma → soft callus → remodelling → hard callus","Haematoma → remodelling → soft callus → hard callus"],"e":"Stages: <b>haematoma formation</b> (clot, inflammation) → <b>fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus</b> (fibroblasts, chondroblasts) → <b>bony (hard) callus</b> (osteoblasts lay woven bone) → <b>remodelling</b> (woven → mature lamellar bone). “Hot Soft Hard Remodel”.","w":["Soft first.","Haematoma first.","Remodelling last.","Remodelling last."],"k":"Haematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodel.","rn":[["","What are the 4 stages of fracture healing?","What are the 4 stages of fracture healing?","Haematoma formation; Fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus; Bony (hard) callus; Remodelling; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10077,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Which two cell types form the fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus? (name either)","e":"Fibroblasts and chondroblasts form the soft callus, later replaced by woven bone (hard callus).","k":"Fibroblasts and chondroblasts.","a":["fibroblasts","chondroblasts","fibroblasts and chondroblasts","fibroblast","chondroblast"],"rn":[["","What forms the fibrocartilaginous callus?","What forms the fibrocartilaginous callus?","Fibroblasts; Chondroblasts"]]},{"id":10078,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"In the bony (hard) callus stage, what replaces the soft callus?","o":["Woven bone laid down by osteoblasts","Lamellar bone laid down by osteoblasts","Woven bone laid down by osteoclasts","Hyaline cartilage laid down by chondroblasts","Fibrocartilage laid down by fibroblasts"],"e":"<b>Osteoblasts</b> replace the fibrocartilaginous callus with <b>woven bone</b>; later <b>remodelling</b> replaces woven bone with <b>mature lamellar bone</b>.","w":["Lamellar comes later.","Osteoclasts resorb.","Not stated.","Soft callus."],"k":"Hard callus = woven bone.","rn":[["","What replaces the fibrocartilaginous callus?","What replaces the fibrocartilaginous callus?","Osteoblasts with woven bone"],["","Which healing stage has hard callus and woven bone?","Which healing stage has hard callus and woven bone?","Bony callus"]]},{"id":10079,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Fracture <b>C</b> has a wedge-shaped third piece. What is it called, and which stage of healing follows the initial haematoma?","o":["Butterfly fragment; fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus","Butterfly fragment; bony (hard) callus","Comminuted; fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus","Oblique; remodelling","Spiral; bony (hard) callus"],"e":"C is a butterfly fragment. Fracture healing: (1) haematoma, (2) fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus formed by fibroblasts and chondroblasts, (3) bony (hard) callus of woven bone, (4) remodelling to lamellar bone.","w":["The hard callus comes after the soft callus.","Comminuted means many fragments (E).","Oblique is a single angled line; remodelling is the last stage.","Spiral winds around the shaft."],"k":"Healing: haematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodelling.","img":{"src":"10/fractures","cap":"Fracture patterns of a long bone. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 4 stages of fracture healing?","What are the 4 stages of fracture healing?","Haematoma formation; Fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus; Bony (hard) callus; Remodelling; MNEMONIC"],["","What is the fibrocartilaginous callus also called?","What is the fibrocartilaginous callus also called?","Soft callus"],["","Which healing stage has new blood vessels and soft callus?","Which healing stage has new blood vessels and soft callus?","Fibrocartilaginous callus"]]},{"id":10080,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Why do children's bones deform and absorb more energy before fracturing?","o":["More flexible bone, thicker periosteum, more cartilage","Stiffer bone, thinner periosteum, more cartilage","More flexible bone, but a thinner periosteum and less cartilage","Fully ossified bone with more yellow marrow","More flexible bone because growth plates have fused"],"e":"Children have <b>more flexible bone</b>, a <b>thicker, more active periosteum</b> and a <b>higher proportion of cartilage</b>, letting bone deform and absorb energy. They <b>heal quickly</b> and may remodel a malunion.","w":["Flexible, thick.","Thicker periosteum, more cartilage.","Not fully ossified.","Plates open."],"k":"Paediatric bone: flexible, thick periosteum, more cartilage.","rn":[["","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","More flexible bone, thicker active periosteum; More cartilage, absorbs more energy; Heal quickly, may remodel malunion; Physeal fractures may affect growth"]]},{"id":10081,"t":"Bone Fractures","f":"sk40","s":"Why are Salter–Harris (physeal) fractures particularly important in children?","o":["They involve the growth plate and may affect growth","They involve the diaphysis and never heal","They involve the periosteum and are always open","They occur only in adults after plate fusion","They involve elastic cartilage of the ear"],"e":"<b>Physeal (growth plate) injuries may affect future growth</b>; they are classified with the <b>Salter–Harris</b> system (types I–V).","w":["Growth plate.","Not stated.","Children.","Not stated."],"k":"Salter–Harris: growth plate fractures.","rn":[["","Image card: What classification of children's fractures is this?","What classification of children's fractures is this?","Salter-Harris classification; Physeal (growth plate) fractures; Types I to V"],["","Physeal fracture","Physeal fracture","Fracture through the growth plate (physis); May affect future growth"]]},{"id":10082,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A child’s femoral fracture was not set correctly and healed at an angle. What is this called?","e":"Malunion: a fracture that heals in an abnormal position.","k":"Malunion.","a":["malunion","mal-union","mal union"],"rn":[["","Image card: What do radiographs a and b show?","What do radiographs a and b show?","Paediatric femoral shaft fracture; Healed in malunion"]]},{"id":10083,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which tissue would you expect to heal most slowly after injury?","o":["Articular hyaline cartilage","Compact bone with periosteum","Spongy bone of the epiphysis","Skeletal muscle","Red marrow"],"e":"<b>Cartilage is avascular</b>, so little oxygen and nutrition reach it, giving poor healing. Bone is vascular and heals (children especially fast).","w":["Vascular.","Vascular.","Muscle heals faster than cartilage.","Vascular."],"k":"Avascular cartilage heals slowest.","rn":[["","Where is hyaline cartilage found besides articular surfaces?","Where is hyaline cartilage found besides articular surfaces?","Bone development and formation"],["","Hyaline cartilage: features and examples?","Hyaline cartilage: features and examples?","Most common, smooth, glassy; Costal cartilages, larynx, articular cartilage"]]},{"id":10084,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"A patient's bone scan shows a balance where osteoclasts are much more active than osteoblasts. Which outcome is expected?","o":["Osteoporosis – weak, porous bone","Osteopetrosis – dense, brittle bone","Acromegaly – excess growth","Rickets – soft bones from low vitamin D","Increased bone density"],"e":"<b>Resorption > formation</b> (osteoclasts outpace osteoblasts) → <b>osteoporosis</b>.","w":["Less resorption.","Hormonal.","Vitamin D.","Lower density."],"k":"Clasts > blasts → osteoporosis.","rn":[["","Osteoporosis","Osteoporosis","Resorption > formation; Weak, porous bone"]]},{"id":10085,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which bone would retain red marrow in an adult?","o":["Sternum","Distal tibia","Distal radius","Phalanges of the toes","Distal femur"],"e":"Adult red marrow: <b>axial skeleton</b> (skull, ribs, <b>sternum</b>, vertebrae), <b>pelvis</b> and <b>proximal</b> ends of humerus and femur.","w":["Distal limb – yellow.","Distal limb.","Distal limb.","Distal end."],"k":"Sternum keeps red marrow.","rn":[["","Which sites keep red marrow in the adult midline skeleton?","Which sites keep red marrow in the adult midline skeleton?","Skull; Ribs; Sternum; Pelvis"]]},{"id":10086,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Name the region labelled <b>A</b>, the expanded articular end of the long bone.","e":"A long bone has an epiphysis at each end, a metaphysis connecting each epiphysis to the shaft, and the diaphysis (shaft). Articular cartilage covers the epiphysis.","k":"A = epiphysis.","img":{"src":"10/longbone","cap":"Adult long bone (humerus). Labels removed."},"a":["epiphysis","proximal epiphysis"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of a developing long bone?","What are the 3 parts of a developing long bone?","Epiphysis; Metaphysis; Diaphysis"],["","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Articular cartilage is typically on what part of a long bone?","Epiphysis"]]},{"id":10087,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which statement best explains why the hip and shoulder can rotate but the elbow cannot?","o":["Hip and shoulder: multiaxial ball and socket; elbow: uniaxial hinge","Hip and shoulder: biaxial condyloid; elbow: uniaxial hinge","Hip and shoulder: multiaxial ball and socket; elbow: uniaxial pivot","Hip and shoulder: plane joints; elbow: hinge","Elbow has no synovial cavity"],"e":"Ball and socket joints are <b>multiaxial</b> (including rotation); hinge joints are <b>uniaxial</b> (flexion and extension only).","w":["Multiaxial.","Hinge.","Ball and socket.","Synovial."],"k":"Multiaxial vs uniaxial.","rn":[["","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, rotation; Hip, shoulder"],["","Which synovial joint is multiaxial?","Which synovial joint is multiaxial?","Ball and socket"]]},{"id":10088,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Which pairing of bone and ossification type is correct?","o":["Clavicle – intramembranous","Mandible – endochondral","Femur – intramembranous","Vertebrae – intramembranous","Cranial vault – endochondral"],"e":"<b>Intramembranous</b>: cranial vault, mandible, <b>clavicle</b>. <b>Endochondral</b>: long bones (femur), vertebrae, pelvis, skull base.","w":["Intramembranous.","Endochondral.","Endochondral.","Intramembranous."],"k":"Clavicle = intramembranous.","rn":[["","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Cranial vault (flat skull bones); Mandible (maxilla/mandible); Clavicle"]]},{"id":10089,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Which fracture healing stage is most closely linked to the pain and swelling immediately after injury?","o":["Haematoma formation","Soft (fibrocartilaginous) callus","Hard (bony) callus","Remodelling","Epiphyseal fusion"],"e":"Stage 1, <b>haematoma formation</b>: a blood clot develops and <b>inflammation begins</b>; pain is detected via the richly innervated periosteum.","w":["Later.","Later.","Last.","Not healing."],"k":"Haematoma + inflammation first.","rn":[["","What happens in haematoma formation?","What happens in haematoma formation?","Blood clot at fracture site; Inflammation begins"]]},{"id":10090,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which structure is a long bone despite being small?","o":["A phalanx","A carpal","The patella","A vertebra","The scapula"],"e":"<b>Phalanges</b> are long bones (length > width). Carpals are short; patella sesamoid; vertebra irregular; scapula flat.","w":["Short.","Sesamoid.","Irregular.","Flat."],"k":"Phalanx = long bone.","rn":[["","What 2 bones form the metacarpophalangeal joint?","What 2 bones form the metacarpophalangeal joint?","Metacarpal; Proximal phalanx"]]},{"id":10091,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about the periosteum is correct?","o":["Outer covering of compact bone, with arteries and nerves","Inner lining of the medullary cavity with many nerves","Outer covering of compact bone with no blood supply","An outer hyaline cartilage covering over the articular surfaces","Outer covering of compact bone, found only in adults"],"e":"<b>Periosteum</b> (“peri” = around): outer connective tissue covering compact bone; <b>periosteal arteries</b> supply outer compact bone; rich in <b>sensory nerves</b>. In children it is thicker and more active.","w":["Endosteum.","Arteries.","Not cartilage.","Thicker in children."],"k":"Periosteum: outer, vascular, innervated.","rn":[["","What do the periosteal arteries supply?","What do the periosteal arteries supply?","The periosteum and outer compact bone"],["","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum: covers the outer compact bone; Endosteum: lines the medullary cavity"]]},{"id":10092,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"A tooth is held in its socket by a joint with little movement. Which classification is correct?","o":["Fibrous joint – gomphosis","Synovial joint – pivot","Cartilaginous joint – symphysis","Fibrous joint – suture","Synovial joint – plane"],"e":"<b>Gomphosis</b>: a peg-in-socket <b>fibrous</b> joint – tooth in alveolar socket.","w":["Not synovial.","Fibrocartilage.","Skull bones.","Not synovial."],"k":"Tooth = gomphosis.","rn":[["","Gomphosis","Gomphosis","Peg in socket fibrous joint"],["","Image card: What type of joint holds these teeth in place?","What type of joint holds these teeth in place?","Gomphosis (peg in socket); Fibrous joint; Tooth in alveolar socket"]]},{"id":10093,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Which statement about the os coxae is correct?","o":["Ilium, ischium and pubis fuse into one bone","Ilium, ischium and sacrum fuse into one bone","It is part of the axial skeleton","It forms by intramembranous ossification","It is a sesamoid bone in a tendon"],"e":"The <b>os coxae</b> starts as <b>ilium, ischium and pubis</b> and fuses into one – an example of ~300 → ~206 bones. It is part of the appendicular pelvic girdle and has the iliac crest and ischial tuberosity.","w":["Pubis.","Appendicular.","Endochondral.","Not sesamoid."],"k":"Os coxae: three bones fuse.","rn":[["","Why does the number of bones decrease?","Why does the number of bones decrease?","Bones ossify and fuse"]]},{"id":10094,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"Which change would most likely increase bone density in an athlete's tibia?","o":["Weight-bearing exercise","Prolonged bed rest","Increased osteoclast activity","Reduced calcium intake","Vitamin D deficiency"],"e":"<b>Wolff's Law</b>: bone adapts to loads; exercised tibiae had denser compact bone.","w":["Reduces.","Resorption.","Reduces.","Reduces."],"k":"Load → denser bone."},{"id":10095,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which pairing of cartilaginous joint and cartilage type is correct?","o":["Intervertebral disc – symphysis – fibrocartilage","Intervertebral disc – synchondrosis – hyaline","Growth plate – symphysis – fibrocartilage","Pubic symphysis – synchondrosis – hyaline","Growth plate – syndesmosis – ligament"],"e":"Intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis: <b>symphysis (fibrocartilage)</b>. Growth plate: <b>synchondrosis (hyaline)</b>.","w":["Symphysis.","Synchondrosis.","Symphysis.","Synchondrosis."],"k":"Disc = symphysis; growth plate = synchondrosis.","rn":[["","The intervertebral discs are which type of cartilage?","The intervertebral discs are which type of cartilage?","Fibrocartilage"],["","Image card: What type of joint is circled at 1 (top) and 2 (bottom)?","What type of joint is circled at 1 (top) and 2 (bottom)?","Symphysis (fibrocartilage); Top: intervertebral disc; Bottom: pubic symphysis"],["","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis vs synchondrosis: cartilage","Symphysis: fibrocartilage; Synchondrosis: hyaline cartilage"]]},{"id":10096,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Name the region labelled <b>C</b>, the shaft of the long bone.","e":"C is the diaphysis (shaft): compact bone around a medullary cavity containing marrow, supplied by the nutrient artery.","k":"C = diaphysis.","img":{"src":"10/longbone","cap":"Adult long bone (humerus). Labels removed."},"a":["diaphysis","shaft"],"rn":[["","What is the diaphysis?","What is the diaphysis?","Shaft of the bone; Ring of cortical bone around the medullary cavity"]]},{"id":10097,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which statement about sutures is correct?","o":["Immobile fibrous skull joints that ossify","Slightly movable cartilaginous joints of the skull","Fibrous joints between the radius and ulna","Fibrocartilage joints between vertebrae","Peg-in-socket joints of the teeth"],"e":"<b>Sutures</b> are immobile <b>fibrous</b> joints between cranial bones (e.g. sagittal suture); a fetal skull shows suture lines that disappear once ossified.","w":["Fibrous and immobile.","Syndesmosis.","Symphysis.","Gomphosis."],"k":"Sutures: fibrous cranial joints.","rn":[["","Suture","Suture","Immobile fibrous joint between cranial bones"]]},{"id":10098,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which bone feature is described as “a projection with a roughened surface”?","o":["Tuberosity","Tubercle","Trochanter","Condyle","Facet"],"e":"<b>Tuberosity</b>: projection with a <b>roughened</b> surface (e.g. ischial tuberosity). Tubercle: small rounded projection.","w":["Small rounded.","Large, femur.","Articular.","Flat surface."],"k":"Tuberosity = rough projection.","rn":[["","What is a tuberosity?","What is a tuberosity?","Projection with a roughened surface"]]},{"id":10099,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Where are fossae usually found?","o":["At joints, where bones articulate or muscles sit","In the skull only, around the foramina","In the medullary cavity of long bones","On the periosteum of flat bones","On the femur only, near the greater and lesser trochanters"],"e":"<b>Fossae</b> are usually at joints: something is about to articulate there, or a muscle sits in it.","w":["Not only.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not only femur."],"k":"Fossae near joints/muscles.","rn":[["","Where do you usually find a fossa?","Where do you usually find a fossa?","Near joints; Where bones articulate or muscles sit"]]},{"id":10100,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which statement about the proximal articular end of a long bone is correct?","o":["The head – technically a condyle too","The epicondyle – a projection above it","The trochanter – a large rough projection","The tubercle – a small rounded projection","The facet – a flat articular surface"],"e":"Condyles are articulating surfaces usually at <b>distal</b> ends; the <b>proximal</b> equivalent is the <b>head</b>. Technically the head is a condyle too, but we call it the head.","w":["Above a condyle.","Femur.","Humerus.","Not the term."],"k":"Proximal articular end = head.","rn":[["","Is the head of the femur technically a condyle?","Is the head of the femur technically a condyle?","Yes, it acts on an axis; It is simply called the head"]]},{"id":10101,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which statement about joint movement description is correct?","o":["We usually describe the joint moving, not the muscle","We usually describe the muscle moving, not the joint","We describe movement by the bone shape","We describe movement by the cartilage type","Joint and muscle ranges are always identical"],"e":"When describing movement we usually refer to the <b>joint</b> moving, not the muscle; the degree of movement can differ slightly.","w":["Joint.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Can differ."],"k":"Describe the joint's movement.","rn":[["","What is a fossa?","What is a fossa?","A depression (dip); Usually at joints"],["","Solid vs cavitated joints: mobility","Solid vs cavitated joints: mobility","Solid: less mobile; Cavitated: more freely moving"],["","Which synovial joints are uniaxial?","Which synovial joints are uniaxial?","Hinge; Pivot; Plane (usually)"]]},{"id":10102,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Which combination best explains why paediatric fractures heal faster?","o":["More red marrow, thicker periosteum, ongoing growth","More yellow marrow, thinner periosteum, ongoing growth","More red marrow and vessels, thinner periosteum, fused growth plates","Less blood supply, thicker periosteum, ongoing growth","More cartilage, absent periosteum, fused growth plates"],"e":"Children heal faster (partly due to <b>more red marrow</b> and vascularisation), have a <b>thicker, more active periosteum</b>, and ongoing growth lets them <b>remodel</b> malunions.","w":["Red marrow.","Thicker periosteum; plates open.","More supply.","Present."],"k":"Why children heal faster.","rn":[["","Why do paediatric fractures heal quickly?","Why do paediatric fractures heal quickly?","Ongoing growth; More red marrow"]]},{"id":10103,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which pairing of axial skeleton component and number is correct?","o":["Ribs – 12 pairs (12 × 2)","Vertebrae – 31","Ribs – 10 pairs","Vertebrae – 26 in adults by the slide count","Ribs – 7 pairs"],"e":"Axial skeleton: skull, <b>vertebrae (33)</b>, <b>ribs (12 × 2)</b>, sternum, hyoid, sacrum, coccyx.","w":["33.","12.","33 on the slide.","12."],"k":"12 pairs of ribs; 33 vertebrae.","rn":[["","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Skull; Vertebrae (33); Ribs (12 pairs); Sternum; Hyoid; Sacrum; Coccyx"]]},{"id":10104,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Which pairing of artery and region is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Periosteal arteries – medullary cavity","Nutrient arteries – marrow","Metaphyseal arteries – metaphysis","Epiphyseal arteries – ends of the bone","Periosteal arteries – outer compact bone"],"e":"<b>Periosteal arteries</b> supply the <b>outer compact bone</b>; the medullary cavity is supplied by <b>nutrient arteries</b>.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Periosteal arteries → outer compact bone.","rn":[["","What does the nutrient artery supply?","What does the nutrient artery supply?","The medullary cavity (marrow)"],["","What do the periosteal arteries supply?","What do the periosteal arteries supply?","The periosteum and outer compact bone"],["","Which 3 arterial systems supply bone?","Which 3 arterial systems supply bone?","Nutrient: medullary cavity, marrow; Metaphyseal, epiphyseal: long bone ends; Periosteal: outer compact bone"]]},{"id":10105,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about the trabeculae is correct?","o":["The struts of the lattice in spongy bone","The channels that connect osteocytes","The rings of osteocytes around a canal","The outer dense layer of compact bone","The fibres in elastic cartilage"],"e":"<b>Trabeculae</b> are the struts of the lattice in <b>spongy</b> bone. Canaliculi connect osteocytes.","w":["Canaliculi.","Osteon.","Compact.","Elastin."],"k":"Trabeculae = struts of spongy bone.","rn":[["","Trabecular bone: structure?","Trabecular bone: structure?","Porous, lightweight lattice of trabeculae"]]},{"id":10106,"t":"Bone Development","s":"Which X-ray finding in a child should <b>not</b> be mistaken for a fracture?","o":["A thin dark line across the end of the bone","A displaced break through the shaft","A spiral line along the shaft after twisting","Multiple fragments after a crush injury","A fragment piercing the skin"],"e":"The <b>growth plate</b> appears as a thin dark line (cartilage not shown on X-ray) – not a fracture. Separate ossification centres also are not fractures.","w":["Fracture.","Fracture.","Fracture.","Fracture."],"k":"Growth plate line ≠ fracture.","rn":[["","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","How does a growth plate look on an x-ray?","Thin dark line"]]},{"id":10107,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"In a 12-year-old, an injury damages the cartilage plate at the junction of regions A and B. What is the main concern?","o":["Disturbed growth in length of the bone (physeal injury)","Loss of red marrow from the diaphysis","Immediate fusion of the epiphysis with no consequence","Infection of the medullary cavity","Failure of intramembranous ossification of the skull"],"e":"Long bones grow in length at the epiphyseal (growth) plates between the epiphysis and metaphysis. Salter–Harris (physeal) fractures matter in children because damage can disturb future growth.","w":["Marrow is in the medullary cavity, not the growth plate.","Premature fusion or damage can stop or distort growth.","Not implied by the injury.","Long bones form by endochondral ossification; the skull is intramembranous."],"k":"Growth plate injury → growth disturbance.","img":{"src":"10/longbone","cap":"Adult long bone (humerus). Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Image card: What classification of children's fractures is this?","What classification of children's fractures is this?","Salter-Harris classification; Physeal (growth plate) fractures; Types I to V"],["","What does the growth plate allow?","What does the growth plate allow?","Bone to keep increasing in length"],["","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","More flexible bone, thicker active periosteum; More cartilage, absorbs more energy; Heal quickly, may remodel malunion; Physeal fractures may affect growth"]]},{"id":10108,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Which combination describes how a long bone grows in length and width during childhood?","o":["At growth plates by endochondral ossification, with vessels and red marrow","At growth plates by intramembranous ossification, with yellow marrow","At the diaphysis by endochondral ossification","By remodelling at the periosteum alone","By fusion of the growth plates"],"e":"Long bones grow in <b>length at the epiphyseal growth plates</b> (endochondral); vascularisation and red marrow support lengthening and increasing diameter. Fusion ends growth.","w":["Endochondral.","Growth plates.","Width/maintenance.","Fusion ends growth."],"k":"Lengthwise growth at physes.","rn":[["","Why do paediatric fractures heal quickly?","Why do paediatric fractures heal quickly?","Ongoing growth; More red marrow"],["","What are the 5 stages of endochondral ossification?","What are the 5 stages of endochondral ossification?","Hyaline cartilaginous template; Template replaced by bone; Different ossification centres emerge; Growth plate between epiphysis and metaphysis; Growth plate narrows, replaced by bone"],["","What are growth plates in children called?","What are growth plates in children called?","Physes (singular physis)"]]},{"id":10109,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about elastic cartilage is correct?","o":["Flexible, with elastin: epiglottis","Strong, resists compression: discs and TMJ","Glassy, most common: articular and costal","Flexible with elastin: discs and TMJ","Vascular with elastin: epiglottis and larynx"],"e":"<b>Elastic</b> cartilage: <b>flexible</b>, contains <b>elastin fibres</b>; epiglottis and auditory tube.","w":["Fibrocartilage.","Hyaline.","Fibrocartilage.","Cartilage is avascular."],"k":"Elastic cartilage features.","rn":[["","Elastic cartilage: features and examples?","Elastic cartilage: features and examples?","Flexible, contains elastin fibres; Epiglottis, auditory tube"]]},{"id":10110,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about the endosteum is correct?","o":["Lining inside the medullary cavity","Covering outside compact bone","Hyaline cartilage at the ends","Growth plate between epiphysis and diaphysis","Layer with the most sensory nerves"],"e":"<b>Endosteum</b>: connective-tissue lining <b>inside</b> the medullary cavity. The periosteum has more sensory nerves.","w":["Periosteum.","Articular.","Physis.","Periosteum."],"k":"Endosteum lines the cavity.","rn":[["","What 3 layers surround the medullary cavity, outside to inside?","What 3 layers surround the medullary cavity, outside to inside?","Periosteum; Compact bone; Endosteum"],["","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum vs endosteum","Periosteum: covers the outer compact bone; Endosteum: lines the medullary cavity"]]},{"id":10111,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"Which joint types are described as having little or no movement?","o":["Fibrous and cartilaginous joints","Ball and socket and condyloid joints","Hinge and pivot joints","Saddle and plane joints","All synovial joints"],"e":"<b>Fibrous</b> and <b>cartilaginous</b> (solid) joints have <b>little or no movement</b>; synovial joints are freely movable.","w":["Synovial.","Synovial.","Synovial.","Freely movable."],"k":"Solid joints: little movement.","rn":[["","What are the 2 types of solid joints?","What are the 2 types of solid joints?","Fibrous; Cartilaginous"]]},{"id":10112,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"A patient's jaw is injured at the TMJ. Based on the cartilage present, what might you expect about healing?","o":["Slow, as fibrocartilage is avascular","Fast, as fibrocartilage is vascular","Slow, as elastic cartilage is avascular","Fast, by intramembranous ossification","No cartilage is involved at the TMJ"],"e":"The TMJ contains <b>fibrocartilage</b>; all cartilage is <b>avascular</b>, so healing is slow.","w":["Avascular.","Fibrocartilage.","Not stated.","Fibrocartilage."],"k":"Cartilage injury heals slowly."},{"id":10113,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"Which statement about lamellar and woven bone in fracture healing is correct?","o":["Woven bone forms first, then remodels into lamellar bone","Lamellar bone forms first, then remodels into woven bone","Woven bone is the final mature bone","Lamellar bone forms in the haematoma","Neither forms during fracture healing"],"e":"Hard callus: osteoblasts lay <b>woven bone</b>; remodelling replaces it with <b>mature lamellar bone</b>, restoring shape and strength.","w":["Woven first.","Lamellar is final.","Not stated.","They do."],"k":"Woven → lamellar.","rn":[["","What happens in the remodelling stage?","What happens in the remodelling stage?","Woven bone replaced by mature lamellar bone; Shape and strength restored"]]},{"id":10114,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which statement about the hyoid bone is correct?","o":["It is part of the axial skeleton","It is part of the appendicular skeleton","It is a sesamoid bone in a tendon","It is part of the pelvic girdle","It is a limb bone"],"e":"The axial skeleton includes the skull, vertebrae, ribs, sternum, <b>hyoid</b>, sacrum and coccyx.","w":["Axial.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Hyoid = axial.","rn":[["","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Which bones make up the axial skeleton?","Skull; Vertebrae (33); Ribs (12 pairs); Sternum; Hyoid; Sacrum; Coccyx"],["","Image card: Spotter 16: are the phalanges part of the axial or appendicular skeleton? (yellow = axi…","Spotter 16: are the phalanges part of the axial or appendicular skeleton? (yellow = axial, green = appendicular)","Appendicular"]]},{"id":10115,"t":"Bone Classification","s":"Which statement about why trochanters are larger than tubercles is given?","o":["Lower limb muscles are larger, needing bigger attachments","Upper limb muscles are larger, needing bigger attachments","The femur bears no muscles, so needs larger projections","Trochanters are articular surfaces for the hip","Tubercles are holes for vessels"],"e":"Trochanters are larger and only on the femur because the <b>lower limb has larger muscles</b> needing bigger attachment points; the upper limb has tubercles.","w":["Lower.","Not stated.","Not articular.","Projections."],"k":"Bigger muscles → bigger projections.","rn":[["","Why are trochanters only on the femur?","Why are trochanters only on the femur?","Lower limb has larger muscles; So needs bigger attachment points"]]},{"id":10116,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"Deficiency of which vitamin causes scurvy and poor wound healing?","e":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy.","k":"Vitamin C.","a":["vitamin c","c","ascorbic acid"],"rn":[["","What does a lack of vitamin C cause?","What does a lack of vitamin C cause?","Poor wound healing; Scurvy (extreme)"],["","What are the 3 vitamins important for bone?","What are the 3 vitamins important for bone?","Vitamin A; Vitamin C; Vitamin D"]]},{"id":10117,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"Which statement about the sacrum is correct?","o":["5 fused vertebrae, axial, joins both sides of the pelvis","5 fused vertebrae, appendicular, forming part of the pelvic girdle","4 fused vertebrae, axial, articulates with both hip bones","5 fused vertebrae, axial, part of the coccyx","A sesamoid bone within the pelvic ligaments"],"e":"The <b>sacrum</b>: <b>5 fused</b> sacral vertebrae, <b>axial</b>, articulating with the right and left pelvis.","w":["The sacrum is axial.","5.","Separate.","Not sesamoid."],"k":"Sacrum: 5 fused, axial.","rn":[["","How many sacral vertebrae are there?","How many sacral vertebrae are there?","5 (fused into 1 sacrum)"]]},{"id":10118,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"What is the unmineralised matrix laid down by osteoblasts called?","e":"Osteoid is unmineralised bone matrix secreted by osteoblasts before mineralisation.","k":"Osteoid.","a":["osteoid"],"rn":[["","Image card: What are these cells and what are they secreting?","What are these cells and what are they secreting?","Osteoblasts; Bone matrix (osteoid)"],["","Osteoid","Osteoid","Bone matrix secreted by osteoblasts; Becomes woven bone"]]},{"id":10119,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A child's growth plate is injured. Which future complication should be considered?","o":["Disturbed future growth","Immediate osteopetrosis","Conversion to elastic cartilage","Loss of all red marrow","Faster fusion of all other plates"],"e":"<b>Physeal injuries may affect future growth</b> (Salter–Harris classification).","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Growth plate injury → growth disturbance.","rn":[["","Physeal fracture","Physeal fracture","Fracture through the growth plate (physis); May affect future growth"]]},{"id":10120,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"Which statement best summarises the role of the three bone cells in fracture healing and remodelling?","o":["Osteoblasts build, osteocytes regulate, osteoclasts remove","Osteoclasts build, osteocytes regulate, osteoblasts remove","Osteoblasts build, osteoclasts regulate, osteocytes remove","Osteocytes build, osteoblasts regulate, osteoclasts remove","Chondrocytes build, osteocytes regulate, osteoclasts remove"],"e":"Summary: <b>osteoblasts build, osteocytes regulate, osteoclasts resorb</b> – working together in remodelling and the final stage of fracture healing.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Swapped.","Not chondrocytes."],"k":"Three bone cells.","rn":[["","Osteoblasts vs osteocytes vs osteoclasts: jobs","Osteoblasts vs osteocytes vs osteoclasts: jobs","Osteoblasts build; Osteocytes regulate; Osteoclasts resorb; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10121,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A 25-year-old man breaks his tibia playing rugby. An X-ray at 1 day shows a blood clot at the fracture site with inflammation. At 2 weeks a temporary stabilising tissue made by fibroblasts and chondroblasts bridges the gap. At 6 weeks woven bone has formed across it.<br><br>What is the next stage, and what does it achieve?","o":["Remodelling: woven bone is replaced by lamellar bone, restoring shape and strength","Haematoma formation: a new clot forms and inflammation begins again","Soft callus: osteoblasts replace woven bone with fibrocartilage","Endochondral ossification: a new cartilage template forms","Epiphyseal fusion: the growth plate closes"],"e":"Step 1: the four stages of fracture healing are haematoma formation, fibrocartilaginous (soft) callus, bony (hard) callus and remodelling; he has passed the first three (clot, fibrocartilage, woven bone). Step 2: the final stage is remodelling, in which woven bone is replaced with mature lamellar bone, restoring the bone's shape and strength.","w":["Haematoma formation is the first stage, which has already happened.","The soft callus came before the woven bone, and osteoblasts make bone, not fibrocartilage.","Healing does not restart a cartilage template; the next stage is remodelling.","Epiphyseal fusion ends growth in length; it is not a stage of healing."],"k":"Healing: haematoma → soft callus → hard callus → remodelling.","rn":[["","What happens in the remodelling stage?","What happens in the remodelling stage?","Woven bone replaced by mature lamellar bone; Shape and strength restored"]]},{"id":10122,"t":"Bone Health and Deficiencies","s":"Two patients have bone problems. Patient 1, a 68-year-old woman past menopause, has weak, porous bones and breaks her wrist after a minor fall. Patient 2, a 20-year-old man, has bones that look very dense on X-ray but are brittle and break easily. Both have an imbalance between the cells that build and remove bone.<br><br>Which imbalance does each patient have?","o":["1: resorption exceeds formation (osteoporosis); 2: decreased resorption (osteopetrosis)","1: decreased resorption (osteopetrosis); 2: resorption exceeds formation (osteoporosis)","1: excess growth hormone (acromegaly); 2: lack of vitamin D (rickets)","Both: osteoblasts are overactive and lay down too much osteoid matrix in the compact bone","1: lack of vitamin C (scurvy); 2: lack of vitamin A"],"e":"Step 1: in osteoporosis, resorption by osteoclasts exceeds formation by osteoblasts, giving weak, porous bone that breaks easily; hormonal changes such as menopause affect bone health. Step 2: in osteopetrosis, resorption is decreased (osteoclasts are under-active), so bone becomes dense but brittle.","w":["The two conditions are swapped.","Acromegaly causes uncontrolled growth and rickets causes soft, bowed bones in children.","Neither problem is described as osteoblast overactivity.","Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and poor wound healing, not these patterns."],"k":"Osteoporosis: resorption > formation; osteopetrosis: resorption ↓.","rn":[["","Osteopetrosis","Osteopetrosis","Decreased resorption; Dense, brittle bone"],["","Osteoporosis","Osteoporosis","Resorption > formation; Weak, porous bone"]]},{"id":10123,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A 12-year-old girl falls from a trampoline and injures her wrist. An X-ray shows a fracture through the band of cartilage between the epiphysis and metaphysis of her radius. Her mother is told the fracture will probably heal quickly but that it needs careful follow-up.<br><br>Why does this fracture need careful follow-up, and how is it classified?","o":["It involves the physis and may affect future growth; Salter–Harris types I–V","It is in avascular cartilage and will never heal; Wolff's Law","It involves the diaphysis and will cause osteopetrosis; open vs closed","It involves the articular cartilage and will become a gomphosis; comminuted vs segmental","It is a fracture of adult bone; transverse or oblique pattern, which does not affect growth in any way"],"e":"Step 1: the band between the epiphysis and metaphysis is the growth plate (physis), where epiphyseal cartilage converts to bone to give longitudinal growth. Step 2: paediatric fractures heal quickly, but physeal (growth plate) injuries may affect future growth, and they are classified with the Salter–Harris system (types I–V).","w":["Children's fractures heal quickly; Wolff's Law is about loading.","The fracture is at the physis, not the diaphysis, and does not cause osteopetrosis.","A gomphosis is a peg-in-socket joint.","She is 12, so her growth plates are still open."],"k":"Physeal fractures may affect growth; classified Salter–Harris I–V.","rn":[["","Physeal fracture","Physeal fracture","Fracture through the growth plate (physis); May affect future growth"],["","Image card: What classification of children's fractures is this?","What classification of children's fractures is this?","Salter-Harris classification; Physeal (growth plate) fractures; Types I to V"]]},{"id":10124,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"A 30-year-old footballer tears the smooth glassy cartilage covering the end of his femur at the knee, and also strains a thigh muscle in the same match. Six weeks later his muscle has recovered but his knee cartilage has barely healed. He asks why the two injuries are recovering at such different speeds.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["Cartilage is avascular, so little oxygen and nutrients reach it","Cartilage has more blood vessels than muscle, so it swells and heals slower","The knee cartilage is elastic cartilage, which cannot repair itself","The cartilage is fibrocartilage, which only heals in the intervertebral discs and the temporomandibular joint","Muscle is avascular, so it heals faster"],"e":"Step 1: the smooth, glassy cartilage covering a joint surface is hyaline (articular) cartilage. Step 2: cartilage is avascular (very little or no blood supply), so little oxygen and nutrition reach it, giving poor wound-healing properties; it heals slowly compared with a stretched muscle.","w":["Cartilage has very little or no blood supply.","Articular cartilage is hyaline; elastic cartilage is in the epiglottis.","Articular cartilage is hyaline, not fibrocartilage.","Muscle heals faster because it is not avascular."],"k":"Cartilage is avascular → heals slowly.","rn":[["","Why does cartilage heal poorly?","Why does cartilage heal poorly?","Avascular; Little oxygen and nutrients reach it"]]},{"id":10125,"t":"Intro to the Skeletal System","s":"A 40-year-old man needs a bone marrow sample to study his blood-cell production. A junior doctor suggests taking it from the middle of his tibia, as is sometimes done in young children. The consultant says the tibial shaft is a poor site in an adult and chooses the pelvis instead.<br><br>Why is the pelvis a better site in this adult?","o":["In adults red marrow is restricted to the axial skeleton, pelvis and proximal humerus and femur","In adults all marrow is red, so any site would work","The pelvis contains only yellow marrow, which makes blood cells","The tibia has no medullary cavity in adults","In adults red marrow is found only in the distal tibia"],"e":"Step 1: red marrow is haematopoietic (makes blood cells), while yellow marrow is mainly adipose tissue for energy storage. Step 2: in children marrow is mostly red and widespread, but in adults red marrow is restricted to the axial skeleton, pelvis and the proximal ends of the humerus and femur; the rest is yellow, so the pelvis is the better site.","w":["In adults much of the marrow is yellow.","Yellow marrow is adipose, not blood-forming.","Long bones keep a medullary cavity with marrow.","The distal tibia is not a listed adult red marrow site."],"k":"Adult red marrow: axial skeleton, pelvis, proximal humerus/femur.","rn":[["","Where is red marrow found in adults?","Where is red marrow found in adults?","Axial skeleton; Pelvis; Proximal ends of humerus and femur"]]},{"id":10126,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"A physiotherapist assesses three joints in a patient. Joint 1 allows only flexion and extension. Joint 2 allows flexion, extension, abduction, adduction and circumduction, but no rotation. Joint 3 allows flexion, extension, abduction, adduction and rotation around many axes.<br><br>Which joints are these most likely to be?","o":["1: elbow (hinge); 2: knuckle (condyloid); 3: hip (ball and socket)","1: atlanto-axial (pivot); 2: elbow (hinge); 3: knuckle (condyloid)","1: shoulder (ball and socket); 2: thumb carpometacarpal (saddle); 3: elbow (hinge)","1: acromioclavicular (plane); 2: hip; 3: atlanto-axial joint (pivot), which rotates the head","1: sagittal suture; 2: pubic symphysis; 3: tooth socket"],"e":"Step 1: a hinge joint is uniaxial, allowing only flexion and extension (elbow); a condyloid joint is biaxial with flexion/extension, abduction/adduction and circumduction but no rotation (metacarpophalangeal joint, the knuckles). Step 2: a ball-and-socket joint is multiaxial, allowing flexion/extension, abduction/adduction and rotation (hip, shoulder).","w":["A pivot joint allows rotation only.","The shoulder is multiaxial, not limited to flexion and extension.","A plane joint glides, and a pivot joint allows only rotation.","These are fibrous and cartilaginous joints with little or no movement."],"k":"Hinge: flex/extend; condyloid: biaxial, no rotation; ball and socket: multiaxial.","rn":[["","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Ball and socket joint: movements and examples?","Flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, rotation; Hip, shoulder"],["","What are the 6 types of synovial joint?","What are the 6 types of synovial joint?","Ball and socket; Condyloid; Saddle; Hinge; Pivot; Plane; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":10127,"t":"Joint Classification","s":"An anatomy student examines three joints in a skeleton. Joint A is a peg sitting in a socket of the jaw. Joint B is between two bones of the forearm and is held together by an interosseous membrane. Joint C joins the two pubic bones with a pad of fibrocartilage.<br><br>Which classification is correct?","o":["A: gomphosis; B: syndesmosis; C: symphysis","A: suture; B: synchondrosis; C: syndesmosis","A: syndesmosis; B: gomphosis; C: synchondrosis","A: gomphosis; B: symphysis; C: synchondrosis, as both pubic bones are joined by hyaline cartilage","A: pivot; B: hinge; C: plane"],"e":"Step 1: fibrous joints have no synovial cavity; a gomphosis is a peg-in-socket joint (tooth in alveolar socket) and a syndesmosis joins bones with a ligament or interosseous membrane (radius and ulna). Step 2: cartilaginous joints include a symphysis (fibrocartilage, e.g. pubic symphysis) and a synchondrosis (hyaline cartilage, e.g. the epiphyseal growth plate).","w":["A suture is between cranial bones, and a synchondrosis uses hyaline cartilage.","The tooth is a gomphosis and the forearm a syndesmosis.","The pubic symphysis is joined by fibrocartilage, not hyaline cartilage.","These are synovial joint types, but none of A–C has a synovial cavity."],"k":"Gomphosis (tooth), syndesmosis (radius–ulna), symphysis (pubic, fibrocartilage).","rn":[["","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture vs syndesmosis vs gomphosis: what joins the bones?","Suture: fibrous tissue; Syndesmosis: ligament/interosseous membrane; Gomphosis: peg in socket"],["","What are the 3 types of fibrous joints?","What are the 3 types of fibrous joints?","Suture; Syndesmosis; Gomphosis"]]},{"id":10128,"t":"Bone Development","s":"A baby is born with a problem in the way cartilage templates are turned into bone, while bones formed directly from undifferentiated stem cells develop normally. Doctors expect some bones to be affected and others to be spared.<br><br>Which bones are most likely affected, and which are spared?","o":["Affected: long bones and vertebrae; spared: cranial vault, mandible and clavicle","Affected: cranial vault, mandible and clavicle; spared: long bones and vertebrae","All bones affected, because every bone forms from a cartilage template","No bones affected, because all bones form from mesenchyme","Affected: only the patella, as sesamoid bones form within a tendon from a cartilage template"],"e":"Step 1: endochondral ossification (\"within cartilage\") starts from a hyaline cartilage template and forms most of the body: long bones, vertebrae, pelvis and skull base. Step 2: intramembranous ossification (\"within membrane\") starts from mesenchyme with no cartilage and forms the flat bones of the skull (cranial vault), the mandible and the clavicle, which would be spared.","w":["The affected and spared groups are swapped.","Flat skull bones, the mandible and clavicle form without cartilage.","Most bones form from a cartilage template.","Endochondral ossification forms far more than the patella."],"k":"Endochondral: long bones, vertebrae; intramembranous: skull vault, mandible, clavicle.","rn":[["","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Which bones form by intramembranous ossification?","Cranial vault (flat skull bones); Mandible (maxilla/mandible); Clavicle"]]},{"id":10129,"t":"Bone Maintenance and Remodelling","s":"A 22-year-old astronaut spends six months in space with very little load on her legs. On return, a scan shows her leg bones are less dense than before. A second athlete who trains daily with heavy weight-bearing exercise has denser compact bone in her legs than average.<br><br>Which principle explains both findings?","o":["Wolff's Law: bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it","Epiphyseal fusion: growth plates close after age 20","Osteopetrosis: decreased resorption makes bone dense but brittle","Bone is not living tissue, so it changes only with ageing over many years","Endochondral ossification: cartilage templates are replaced by bone after loading"],"e":"Step 1: Wolff's Law states that bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it. Step 2: lack of loading (or exercise) reduces bone density, as in sheep studies where exercised tibiae had denser compact bone than controls, while regular loading increases it.","w":["Fusion ends growth in length and does not explain loss or gain of density.","Osteopetrosis is a disorder of reduced resorption, not a response to load.","Bone is living tissue that adapts and remodels.","Endochondral ossification is how bones develop, not how they adapt to load."],"k":"Wolff's Law: bone adapts to load.","rn":[["","Wolff's law","Wolff's law","Bone adapts to the mechanical loads placed upon it"]]},{"id":10130,"t":"Bone Fractures","s":"A 7-year-old boy and his 45-year-old father fall from the same height while skiing. The father breaks his forearm cleanly, while the boy's forearm bone bends and cracks without breaking completely. The boy's fracture heals faster, and a slight angle in it straightens over the next year.<br><br>Which features of children's bone explain these differences?","o":["More flexible bone, thicker active periosteum, more cartilage, and remodelling with growth","Less cartilage and a thinner periosteum, so bone bends","Children have no growth plates, so they remodel faster","Children's bones are more brittle, as in osteopetrosis","Children's fractures never involve remodelling"],"e":"Step 1: children have more flexible bone, a thicker and more active periosteum and a higher proportion of cartilage, which lets bone deform and absorb more energy before it fractures. Step 2: paediatric fractures heal quickly and may remodel a malunion because of ongoing growth, so the slight angle straightens.","w":["Children have more cartilage and a thicker periosteum.","Children do have growth plates (physes).","Children's bone is more flexible, not brittle.","Children's fractures can remodel a malunion with growth."],"k":"Children: flexible bone, thick periosteum, more cartilage, remodel with growth.","rn":[["","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","What are the 4 points about fractures in children?","More flexible bone, thicker active periosteum; More cartilage, absorbs more energy; Heal quickly, may remodel malunion; Physeal fractures may affect growth"]]}]);
